going through each one of the
functions on that cheat sheet,

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:50.850
which altogether probably
comprise about 80%

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:54.060
of every function you'll
ever need to use in R

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:57.230
as an ecologist or
biologist more generally.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.290
So let's get started.

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.900
First we're just going to
create some data really quick

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:04.750
that we can use for demonstrating
all of these functions.

00:01:15.110 --> 00:01:16.260
And there we have it.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:19.660
So here we have a little
bit of data that we can use

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:22.420
to understand how these functions work.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:24.510
So don't worry I'm gonna
include all of this code

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.410
down in the comments below,

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.450
or in the blog posts
that I'm gonna be posting

00:01:29.450 --> 00:01:30.400
with this video,

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.200
so you can just copy and paste this

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.490
into the start of your code.

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:35.050
All right, now let's get to it.

00:01:35.050 --> 00:01:37.830
So the first function is
probably the most common function

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.160
you'll ever need to know in R,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:41.610
and that is the concatenate function

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.860
or just C function for short,

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.640
and this is for creating vectors.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:47.980
So as we did above here
with number vector,

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.900
and so when we run number vector,

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.790
we get these values and we
can also create a vector

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:55.320
using character variables.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:57.200
So here we have SPP one,

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.157
or species one, species three, species two

00:01:59.157 --> 00:02:00.620
and species three.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:02.770
And when we created this
and run species vector,

00:02:02.770 --> 00:02:06.430
we have this vector of character values.

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.453
Okay, next we have sum.

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.200
So with sum, that simply adds up

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:16.230
all the different numbers
in a vector together.

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:20.790
So if we do sum num vec,
and run that, we get 20,

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:23.490
because three plus six plus
three plus eight is 20.

00:02:23.490 --> 00:02:25.610
If we have an NA in these data,

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.350
so let's say we had a vector inside here

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.937
that contains everything in num vec,

00:02:29.937 --> 00:02:33.430
but then also an NA
value or a missing value,

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:36.080
you'll actually get an NA because

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.690
by definition NA means you
don't know what that value is,

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.940
so because you don't know
what one of these values is,

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.993
you cannot actually calculate
the sum of all of these

00:02:42.993 --> 00:02:44.870
and you get an NA.

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.000
However, you can add in an argument,

00:02:47.000 --> 00:02:50.110
NA dot remove or NA dot RM equals true.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:52.120
And when we run this, it ignores that NA

00:02:52.120 --> 00:02:54.340
and sums everything else together.

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:56.220
Next, we have length.

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:59.380
This simply tells you either
the length of a vector,

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:01.538
or the number of columns in a data frame.

00:03:01.538 --> 00:03:05.573
So for, if we do num vec in here,

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.290
we get four because there are
four elements in that vector.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:13.590
If we look at this data
frame here that we created,

00:03:13.590 --> 00:03:14.830
let's just take a look at it,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:17.770
running that, we have
one column of num vec,

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.318
one column of species vec,

00:03:19.318 --> 00:03:20.950
and this is the whole data frame together.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.230
Now, if we run length for the data frame,

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.370
we get just the number of columns,

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.330
in this case, two columns.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:30.570
All right, moving on.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:31.403
Unique.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:36.350
Unique tells us how many
unique elements there are

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:38.150
within a vector.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:43.143
So for example, if we put
in number vec, or num vec,

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.470
we have a three, we have a
six and we have an eight.

00:03:47.470 --> 00:03:49.490
And the reason we don't
have four values here

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:52.180
is because the three repeats itself twice.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.430
Sometimes you want to know what the

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:57.367
total number of unique
values within a vector are.

00:03:57.367 --> 00:03:58.650
And a simple way to do that,

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:01.240
let's say we want to know how
many unique species there are

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:02.670
inside species vec.

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:04.663
So let's put species vec in here.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.940
And when we run that we get
these unique species IDs,

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.320
but let's say we had a really long list

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:11.900
and we can't just count that it's three,

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.030
we can actually use the length function

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.660
around the unique function,

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:16.890
and now we can find out

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:19.010
that there are three unique
different types of species

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.337
inside species vec.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:24.450
All right, next, as dot numeric.

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.120
This is useful because sometimes
when you load data into R,

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.170
for whatever reason it turns a,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:32.640
what should be a number vector
into, or a numeric vector

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:34.520
into a character vector.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:36.363
So let's say for example,

00:04:39.005 --> 00:04:40.290
we had a character vector,

00:04:40.290 --> 00:04:42.270
actually, it was supposed
to be a number of vector,

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:45.803
but actually it somehow loaded into R as.

00:04:49.890 --> 00:04:51.390
And the reason this can happen

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.693
is because I'll show you another example.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:01.543
Let's say these are all numeric,

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:05.840
but then somewhere in your data,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:10.080
your field assistant
entered in the letter O

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:13.580
instead of the number zero.

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.690
And so when you upload these data

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:18.360
and take a look at char vec,

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:21.190
it actually turns all of
these numbers into characters,

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.000
including that original O.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:27.163
So if we use as dot
numeric, put that in there,

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.390
we'll get this warning message that says

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:31.770
NAs introduced by coercion

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:35.660
and that's because that O
isn't actually a real number.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:37.260
In fact, it's not a fake number either.

00:05:37.260 --> 00:05:38.110
It's not a number.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.640
So we are left with these numbers here

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:42.050
three, five, two, one, five

00:05:42.050 --> 00:05:44.800
and an NA value for
where that O used to be.

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:47.950
Next, we have the log function.

00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:50.400
I'm not going to give a full example

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:51.810
of using the log function here.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:53.680
Simply because we don't have a lot of data

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.720
for this quick demonstration,

00:05:55.720 --> 00:05:59.160
but simply put this extracts
the natural logarithm

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:02.120
of a value or of the values in a vector.

00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:03.940
So if we put in here,

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.450
let's just say the number
three and run that,

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:10.870
this is the natural
log of three, 1.098612.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.460
If we have a vector values here, num vec,

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:19.720
it calculates the logarithm
of all of these numbers.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:22.120
And this is useful usually
when you're visualizing

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.120
or plotting data that's skewed to one side

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:28.060
and you want to be able to
visualize it a little bit nicer

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.430
I find myself using log
function quite a bit.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.330
And then you just have to
indicate on one of your axis

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:36.000
that the data are logged transformed.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:38.550
Next we have the sort function.

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.900
This is used to arrange
a vector numerically

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:43.330
or alphabetically.

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.280
So if we have our num
vec here and run that,

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.360
it'll rearrange all the values in num vec,

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.750
starting with the lowest,
going up to the highest,

00:06:51.750 --> 00:06:56.520
or if we do use our species vector,

00:06:56.520 --> 00:06:58.910
it will organize them alphabetically.

00:06:58.910 --> 00:07:02.530
And actually it also
organized them numerically

00:07:02.530 --> 00:07:05.670
because SPP is the same
for all of these values,

00:07:05.670 --> 00:07:08.270
but the one, two, three and three

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:10.160
was used for rearranging these values.

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:11.737
But if let's say we had a

00:07:14.520 --> 00:07:16.083
bunch of different letters,

00:07:19.810 --> 00:07:22.630
D S and A, if we run sort,

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:24.630
whoops, we actually need
to make that into a vector.

00:07:24.630 --> 00:07:26.030
There we go.

00:07:26.030 --> 00:07:27.140
Try that again.

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:29.020
It has rearranged them
with A starting first

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.130
then D and then S.

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:32.930
we can also reverse that order by saying,

00:07:32.930 --> 00:07:35.790
so if we ran sort on num vec,

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:37.270
we get three, three, six, eight

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:42.270
and we can add in this argument,
decreasing equals true.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:46.780
And now the values will be decreasing,

00:07:46.780 --> 00:07:48.900
starting with eight, six, three and three.

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:53.410
Next we have this is dot NA function.

00:07:53.410 --> 00:07:55.260
is dot NA is super useful.

00:07:55.260 --> 00:07:56.710
I use it almost all the time,

00:07:56.710 --> 00:07:58.810
because we're always encountering values

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:02.490
that might be a NA or missing.

00:08:02.490 --> 00:08:04.460
Essentially what this does is it tells us

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:06.340
is a value an NA or not.

00:08:06.340 --> 00:08:09.930
So if we add in a vector
here, let's make up,

00:08:09.930 --> 00:08:11.200
let's make up a vector.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:15.000
One, two, three, five,

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.653
there was no two. It's just
one, three, five NA, run that.

00:08:20.630 --> 00:08:22.820
We get false, false, false, and true.

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:26.190
Because one, three and five
are not NA but NA is NA.

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:28.070
So we get a true there.

00:08:28.070 --> 00:08:30.570
The reason this is useful is normally

00:08:30.570 --> 00:08:32.920
for most other values we could say,

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:35.810
let's say we want to find out,

00:08:35.810 --> 00:08:38.523
actually, let's create
this as a new vector.

00:08:39.915 --> 00:08:40.953
We'll call it new vec.

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:45.443
And let's say,

00:08:45.443 --> 00:08:50.300
we want to see which of these
values is greater than two.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:56.367
So we'll say new vec is greater than two,

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:00.310
and we get a false, true, true, and NA

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:01.990
'cause we don't know what that value is.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:03.810
The three and five are greater than two.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:05.720
The one is less than two. So we get false.

00:09:05.720 --> 00:09:08.570
Let's say we want to see which
values are equal to three.

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:12.270
We get false.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:15.330
And then we get true for the
three, false and then NA,

00:09:15.330 --> 00:09:16.163
that makes sense.

00:09:16.163 --> 00:09:18.230
But now let's see if we want to find out

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:20.020
which values are NA.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:21.573
So we put equals NA.

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:25.410
Ah, but look, we get four NAs,

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.460
that's because NA means
we don't know what it is.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:31.130
So you can't actually
say new vec equals NA,

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:34.650
because that's like saying new
vec equals I don't know what.

00:09:34.650 --> 00:09:38.230
So that's what we use this is dot NA for.

00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.980
That's useful if you want to
identify which values are NA

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.110
and want to remove them
from your data set.

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:47.260
Oftentimes I'll use is dot
NA with an exclamation point

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.990
in front of it, which
actually makes it not.

00:09:50.990 --> 00:09:54.100
So rather than giving you
false, false, false, true.

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.400
It reverses those by
applying this not statement.

00:09:57.400 --> 00:10:00.180
And when you run that you
get true, true, true false.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:01.680
So sometimes for example,

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:03.850
if you wanna remove NAs from a data set,

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:07.270
you can use this and index
your data using this.

00:10:07.270 --> 00:10:08.583
So let's,

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:12.310
we'll actually use new
vec here instead of that

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:14.060
to make it a little simpler to see.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:21.300
If we index new vec with
this true, true, true, false,

00:10:22.350 --> 00:10:24.460
we are left with just one, three and five

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.223
and successfully removed
that NA value from our data.

00:10:28.680 --> 00:10:30.060
All right, next,

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:31.540
we have the

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:35.690
in percent

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:38.930
in percent operator or
whatever that's called.

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.370
Not technically a function,

00:10:40.370 --> 00:10:42.407
but super, super useful nonetheless.

00:10:42.407 --> 00:10:46.230
Used for finding what items
match between two vectors.

00:10:46.230 --> 00:10:49.880
Usually used for matching character values

00:10:49.880 --> 00:10:52.730
between two vectors, but can
be used for numbers as well.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.220
Let me show you how this works.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.900
Let's use the example
from the cheat sheet.

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:03.510
Species vec in,

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:07.903
and let's create this
new vector species one

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:12.410
and species four.

00:11:12.410 --> 00:11:16.050
So what this does is it says
here we've got species vector,

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.090
we've got this vector four different,

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.840
well, three different species,
but four different elements.

00:11:20.840 --> 00:11:22.260
And then we have this other vector

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:25.780
of these two individuals here.

00:11:25.780 --> 00:11:29.050
And what this says is within species vec,

00:11:29.050 --> 00:11:34.050
which of these values are
also found inside this vector.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:37.190
So when we run this, we get
true, false, false, false.

00:11:37.190 --> 00:11:40.211
When we look at what species vector is,

00:11:40.211 --> 00:11:42.500
the first value is species one,

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:45.530
which is found in this
vector, so that's true.

00:11:45.530 --> 00:11:48.080
And species three, species
two and species three

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.930
are not found inside this vector.

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:52.380
So we get false, false,
false for each of those.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.170
Now, if we reverse this

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.210
and run that we get true and false,

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:06.280
because again, species one
is found inside species vec

00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:08.690
but species four is not.

00:12:08.690 --> 00:12:12.310
So remember that whatever goes
before the percent in percent

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:16.000
is the vector for which you're
going to get trues and falses

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.070
to see whether these elements
also occur in this vector.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:22.980
So next, we have a really
cool set of functions

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.440
for parsing date values.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.650
Up until now, we've been
using the base package,

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:29.570
which comes installed with R,

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.830
we don't have to load any new packages,

00:12:31.830 --> 00:12:33.810
but for this next function,

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:37.500
we're going to load
the Lubri date package.

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:38.920
If it's not installed already

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:43.920
use install dot packages Lubridate,

00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:47.370
I have it installed already
so I'm not going to run that.

00:12:47.370 --> 00:12:49.980
So I'm just going to run library

00:12:52.420 --> 00:12:53.253
lubridate

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:56.790
to load that package.

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:58.050
So what lubridate gives us

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.040
is this really cool set of functions

00:13:00.040 --> 00:13:05.040
that we can create
using a Y, an M and a D.

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:09.591
Y stands for year, M stands
for month, D stands for day.

00:13:09.591 --> 00:13:12.920
So we can create a function Y month day,

00:13:12.920 --> 00:13:16.150
we can say day, month, year,

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:20.790
we can say, year, day,
month, you get the idea.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.690
You can do any combination of these three.

00:13:22.690 --> 00:13:25.810
And so you want to order
the year, date and month

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.750
based on the date that
you are trying to parse.

00:13:28.750 --> 00:13:30.970
So let's say we have,

00:13:30.970 --> 00:13:33.960
let's say we somehow
entered in our field data,

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:35.780
the dates, as let's say,

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.870
2016 slash

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:40.970
June

00:13:40.970 --> 00:13:42.590
comma

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:43.920
13.

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.840
Now this is just a character
value, nothing special,

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:48.270
no date object here,

00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:50.440
but let's say all of our
data are in this format

00:13:50.440 --> 00:13:51.760
and you're going, geez,

00:13:51.760 --> 00:13:53.320
how am I going to make R know

00:13:53.320 --> 00:13:54.710
that these are actually date values

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:57.560
and not some weird format
that I decided to use?

00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.320
Well, we'll use this
function here in this case,

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:05.960
Y for year first, month
comes next, date comes last,

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.410
make sure this order is the same

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:10.800
as the order of values in
here and we run it and voila.

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:12.470
We now have a date object

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:14.830
where R knows that this is a date,

00:14:14.830 --> 00:14:18.030
and we can tell that because
it's transformed this format

00:14:18.030 --> 00:14:20.890
into this nice standard date format in R.

00:14:20.890 --> 00:14:25.280
We can also save this
as an object, my date,

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:26.923
and take a look at that.

00:14:29.540 --> 00:14:31.810
If you run the function class

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:33.300
and then put my date inside there,

00:14:33.300 --> 00:14:36.560
we can see that it is now a date object.

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:38.420
So what's really cool
about these functions

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:40.080
is it will take just about

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:43.060
any type of date formatting
you can think of.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:45.830
So let's say another
example from the cheat sheet

00:14:45.830 --> 00:14:48.120
is 13th

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:53.580
of June,

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:55.420
2016.

00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:59.560
In this case, we'll say day,
month, and year, run that,

00:14:59.560 --> 00:15:01.600
and we get that same date object.

00:15:01.600 --> 00:15:04.820
Next, we have a few cool
functions for creating vectors.

00:15:04.820 --> 00:15:08.440
We have the sequence function, S E Q

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.360
and it's used to create a vector of values

00:15:11.360 --> 00:15:14.100
that increment at a regular rate.

00:15:14.100 --> 00:15:17.030
So for example, we could
say takes a few arguments.

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:18.093
So from,

00:15:20.450 --> 00:15:23.463
to and by.

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:26.060
So from,

00:15:26.060 --> 00:15:30.720
let's say we want to go from zero to 10

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.533
and we want to go by twos.

00:15:33.420 --> 00:15:34.410
And when we run that,

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:37.460
we get the values zero,
two, four, six, eight, 10.

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:40.663
If we had put in 0.5 here,

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.080
it would also start at
zero and go up to 10

00:15:44.080 --> 00:15:46.153
but in increments of 0.5.

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:51.460
Then we have the function R E P or repeat.

00:15:51.460 --> 00:15:52.800
And this is used to create a vector

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:55.390
with a repeating set of values.

00:15:55.390 --> 00:15:59.030
So we could say, first
argument is called X.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:03.530
Let's say X is all the
values from one to three.

00:16:03.530 --> 00:16:05.310
So remember if you just look at that,

00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:06.760
it's just one, two and three.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:12.290
And so we'll repeat that times equals two.

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:14.940
So that will take this
pattern here, repeat it twice.

00:16:14.940 --> 00:16:16.180
So now we get a vector that goes

00:16:16.180 --> 00:16:18.300
one, two, three, one, two, three.

00:16:18.300 --> 00:16:21.350
If instead of times, however, we say

00:16:23.310 --> 00:16:24.880
each equals two,

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:27.800
it will take each individual
element of this vector,

00:16:27.800 --> 00:16:30.333
repeat it, and then go
on to the next element.

00:16:31.190 --> 00:16:34.940
So now we have one, one,
two, two, three, three.

00:16:34.940 --> 00:16:36.296
And finally,

00:16:36.296 --> 00:16:37.129
to complete the basic functions.

00:16:37.129 --> 00:16:38.850
I'm gonna go over one of
my favorite functions,

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:43.850
which is the grepl function, G R E P L.

00:16:44.260 --> 00:16:46.310
And this is used for
finding which elements

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:50.750
of a character vector contain
a particular string of text.

00:16:50.750 --> 00:16:53.473
So for example, we could say,

00:16:54.396 --> 00:16:55.550
let's say,

00:16:55.550 --> 00:16:58.280
so the first argument is the
pattern we're searching for.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:16:59.970
So let's say we're searching for

00:16:59.970 --> 00:17:02.800
anything that contains this number three

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:04.550
in the character string

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:09.183
and we'll search within
the vector of species vec.

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:13.393
So let's take a look at that.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:16.430
Look at species vec again,
we have species one,

00:17:16.430 --> 00:17:18.850
species three, species two, species three.

00:17:18.850 --> 00:17:20.610
Species three and species three

00:17:20.610 --> 00:17:23.720
each contain that value
of three inside it.

00:17:23.720 --> 00:17:28.273
So we get a return back that
goes false, true, false, true.

00:17:29.450 --> 00:17:34.020
If for example, we had said spp now,

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:39.020
or maybe just sp, which of these
contain that string of text

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:41.870
we'll get true for everything
of course and so on.

00:17:41.870 --> 00:17:44.050
So common way that I
use the grepl function

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:47.000
is for subsetting data frames or vectors.

00:17:47.000 --> 00:17:51.670
So for example, we could say,
if we look at data frame,

00:17:51.670 --> 00:17:53.410
this is the data frame we created before.

00:17:53.410 --> 00:17:56.696
It has a column of num
vec and species vec.

00:17:56.696 --> 00:18:01.696
And if we want to subset
this, let's say grepl,

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:06.850
let's say we want to extract
all of species three,

00:18:06.850 --> 00:18:08.470
or all the species that contain,

00:18:08.470 --> 00:18:10.680
the species names that
contain the value of three.

00:18:10.680 --> 00:18:12.930
We'll say three here,

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:14.173
just like we did before.

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:18.733
And then data frame,

00:18:20.850 --> 00:18:22.820
we could have just put in
species vec on its own,

00:18:22.820 --> 00:18:24.810
but since we're subsetting
this data frame,

00:18:24.810 --> 00:18:27.080
it's better practice
to include a reference

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:30.110
to the column within the data
frame that we're filtering by.

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:33.660
So, again, if we just
take a look at this part,

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:36.250
run that, we get false, true, false, true.

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:39.290
True for species three and species three.

00:18:39.290 --> 00:18:41.010
And to filter this data frame,

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:42.390
we also add a comma here

00:18:42.390 --> 00:18:45.410
because these are all of
the rows we want to keep,

00:18:45.410 --> 00:18:48.190
and we're gonna apply
this to all the columns.

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:51.200
And there it is.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.270
Now we have filtered this data frame

00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:55.420
down to just species three.

00:18:55.420 --> 00:18:58.470
So that's it for the basic functions.

00:18:58.470 --> 00:19:02.640
Now let's continue on with loading data.

00:19:02.640 --> 00:19:05.340
I don't have any data sets to
load for this quick tutorial,

00:19:05.340 --> 00:19:08.920
but I'll just quickly show
you how these functions work.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:10.580
Read dot CSV.

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:14.230
All you have to do is put in the path for

00:19:14.230 --> 00:19:15.600
the data set you're trying to load.

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:17.800
So actually to make this work

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:19.220
and a little bit nicer for you,

00:19:19.220 --> 00:19:21.030
let's actually reverse those functions.

00:19:21.030 --> 00:19:23.600
Let's use write dot CSV

00:19:25.010 --> 00:19:28.310
to save our special data
frame that we created here,

00:19:28.310 --> 00:19:31.060
and then reload it in using read dot CSV.

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:35.210
So we'll just save that
as data frame dot CSV

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:38.920
and the first argument
of the write dot CSV

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:41.480
is the actual data frame.

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:43.570
In this case, we called it data frame

00:19:43.570 --> 00:19:47.660
and it will save right
in the base directory

00:19:47.660 --> 00:19:50.020
where we are working.

00:19:50.020 --> 00:19:51.680
So to get a look at that,

00:19:51.680 --> 00:19:53.860
let's go on ahead to
actually another function,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:56.690
the get working directory,

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:57.723
or getwd.

00:20:02.660 --> 00:20:05.880
This will show us where
our base working directory

00:20:05.880 --> 00:20:07.510
is located.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:10.670
Here in my case, it's located in this

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:13.700
user's lukanegoita,
documents, entrepreneurship,

00:20:13.700 --> 00:20:16.910
grad ecologists, YouTube
R tutorials folder,

00:20:16.910 --> 00:20:20.040
which is actually this folder right here.

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:24.010
Let's say we want to save
it inside this folder here,

00:20:24.010 --> 00:20:25.720
which is tutorial scripts.

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:27.570
So I'm just going to add in here,

00:20:27.570 --> 00:20:31.573
tutorial scripts slash data frame dot CSV,

00:20:35.330 --> 00:20:37.143
and it will save right in here.

00:20:39.210 --> 00:20:40.860
There it is.

00:20:40.860 --> 00:20:44.230
All right, so now let's
use this read dot CSV

00:20:44.230 --> 00:20:46.210
to load it back in.

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:49.503
We have to use this
same file path location,

00:20:51.110 --> 00:20:54.520
and we'll save it into a new file,

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:59.520
let's call it data frame uploaded.

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:04.690
Now let's take a look
at data frame uploaded,

00:21:04.690 --> 00:21:06.020
and there it is.

00:21:06.020 --> 00:21:07.080
Now, interestingly,

00:21:07.080 --> 00:21:10.500
it added this extra column
of one, two, three, four

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:13.540
and that's just because
when you use write dot CSV,

00:21:13.540 --> 00:21:18.540
it helps to add in row
dot names equals false.

00:21:18.940 --> 00:21:19.773
'Cause for some reason,

00:21:19.773 --> 00:21:22.330
write dot CSV likes to
add in these row names

00:21:22.330 --> 00:21:24.370
whenever you're saving a data frame.

00:21:24.370 --> 00:21:28.250
So if we run that again and now load

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:31.540
this data frame one more
time and take a look.

00:21:31.540 --> 00:21:34.390
Now we have only those
original two columns

00:21:34.390 --> 00:21:35.823
that we had before.

00:21:36.740 --> 00:21:37.870
Great.

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:41.630
Now, we have this other function
we haven't talked about,

00:21:41.630 --> 00:21:44.200
set working directory.

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:46.880
This is to set where the working
directory is going to be.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:48.790
In this case, we used
get working directory

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:49.860
to see where it was.

00:21:49.860 --> 00:21:51.870
And if we wanted to change
where the working directory is,

00:21:51.870 --> 00:21:52.880
we could use that.

00:21:52.880 --> 00:21:55.533
However, I include it
here to make a point.

00:21:56.610 --> 00:21:58.940
It's a really important and good idea

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:01.970
not to ever use set working directory.

00:22:01.970 --> 00:22:06.290
Instead, go to file
new project and create,

00:22:06.290 --> 00:22:08.083
oh, let's save that, okay.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:11.790
New directory or existing directory,

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.770
new project, directory name,

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.090
create that project

00:22:15.090 --> 00:22:18.130
and then start creating scripts in there.

00:22:18.130 --> 00:22:19.290
You can see here,

00:22:19.290 --> 00:22:22.120
I have a project called
YouTube R tutorials.

00:22:22.120 --> 00:22:24.820
The great thing about
using R studio projects

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:27.790
is that it creates that base directory

00:22:27.790 --> 00:22:32.610
right where the YouTube R
tutorial's project file is based.

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:35.060
So if we go back one folder
and scroll down here,

00:22:35.060 --> 00:22:38.880
you'll see I have this YouTube
R tutorials dot r proj file.

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.220
And that's automatically created

00:22:40.220 --> 00:22:42.700
when you create a new project in R.

00:22:42.700 --> 00:22:45.590
The nice thing about this
is now if I take this

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.280
entire folder with the R project in it

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:51.170
and email it to you,
share it with a friend,

00:22:51.170 --> 00:22:53.460
send it wherever I wanna send it

00:22:53.460 --> 00:22:55.300
and you run this same script,

00:22:55.300 --> 00:22:59.150
you will also be able
to load up these data

00:22:59.150 --> 00:23:01.700
from this tutorial scripts folder,

00:23:01.700 --> 00:23:03.800
because the base directory,

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:05.810
this part, when you use
get working directory,

00:23:05.810 --> 00:23:08.030
this part of everything will change

00:23:08.030 --> 00:23:10.810
depending on your computer and
where you saved this project.

00:23:10.810 --> 00:23:12.970
But YouTube R tutorials,

00:23:12.970 --> 00:23:15.900
that base directory
will always be the same

00:23:15.900 --> 00:23:20.240
and relative to whoever
is using that project.

00:23:20.240 --> 00:23:22.550
So my spiel is done.

00:23:22.550 --> 00:23:23.960
Don't you set working directory,

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:26.050
use R project files,

00:23:26.050 --> 00:23:28.970
and let's go on from there.

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:32.930
Next, let's create some custom functions.

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:33.763
So up until now,

00:23:33.763 --> 00:23:35.900
we've been using functions
that are already created

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:36.860
and built in with R

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:38.870
or other packages that
contain these functions.

00:23:38.870 --> 00:23:41.870
But sometimes you just need to
create that perfect function

00:23:41.870 --> 00:23:46.120
that fits your job exactly
as you need to do it.

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:48.470
And there's just nothing else out there.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:50.250
So here we go.

00:23:50.250 --> 00:23:51.920
So to create a function,

00:23:51.920 --> 00:23:54.603
you use the function function.

00:23:56.860 --> 00:24:00.030
Let's name this function, my

00:24:02.210 --> 00:24:03.190
func.

00:24:03.190 --> 00:24:04.340
And by the way,

00:24:04.340 --> 00:24:08.260
if you hear something that
sounds like a dying dog outside,

00:24:08.260 --> 00:24:11.040
that is actually a sea
lion, that's not dying.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:14.420
That's actually quite happy and
roaming around in the water,

00:24:14.420 --> 00:24:16.570
defending its territory.

00:24:16.570 --> 00:24:19.410
Just wanted to alleviate any anxiety

00:24:19.410 --> 00:24:21.850
that that sound might be giving you.

00:24:21.850 --> 00:24:24.580
I'm based in the Galapagos
very fortunately.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:28.180
So, and so there are
random wildlife sounds

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:31.480
that will fill this tutorial as well.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:32.700
Just a heads up there.

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.660
Okay, now back to the, my function.

00:24:35.660 --> 00:24:38.020
I'm calling it my func and into that,

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:40.950
we're putting this function function.

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.130
The arguments of the function function

00:24:43.130 --> 00:24:47.190
are whatever arguments you
want your function to take in.

00:24:47.190 --> 00:24:50.400
So we'll just call the
simplest way to do it

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:51.510
is we'll just call it X.

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:53.140
We could've called it whatever we wanted.

00:24:53.140 --> 00:24:56.840
So our function will take
in one value that's called X

00:24:56.840 --> 00:24:58.560
or one variable that's called X.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:25:01.160
And then you use these curly brackets

00:25:01.160 --> 00:25:03.800
to enter the contents of the function.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:06.850
Let's say we want a function
that will modify the value of X

00:25:06.850 --> 00:25:08.460
in this weird way.

00:25:08.460 --> 00:25:11.303
So we'll create this variable X mod,

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:15.710
and to modify it, to create X mod,

00:25:15.710 --> 00:25:19.410
we're gonna take X, add five to it,

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:22.680
and then take the result of
that and multiply it by three.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:25.490
There's one more thing we
have to add to this function

00:25:25.490 --> 00:25:28.040
and that is a return function.

00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:29.500
This function tells R

00:25:29.500 --> 00:25:32.100
what aspect of the inside of this function

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:35.490
to actually return or output to the user.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:38.473
In this case, we're going to say X mod.

00:25:39.340 --> 00:25:41.020
So looking through this,

00:25:41.020 --> 00:25:42.840
we've got this function function

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:45.470
where it will take this value of X,

00:25:45.470 --> 00:25:49.520
and then it will take
that value of X add five,

00:25:49.520 --> 00:25:51.750
multiply that whole thing by three,

00:25:51.750 --> 00:25:53.310
save that as X mod,

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:56.560
and then the function returns
the contents of X mod.

00:25:56.560 --> 00:25:59.460
So if we run this, nothing happens,

00:25:59.460 --> 00:26:02.750
but the function is now saved here

00:26:02.750 --> 00:26:05.150
in our environment under
the functions category.

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:08.570
And so to run this function,

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:10.630
all we have to do is use my func

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:12.773
and now we can use parentheses with it

00:26:12.773 --> 00:26:14.450
just like you would
with any other function.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:16.970
And the first argument that it takes,

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:19.470
the only argument that it takes is X.

00:26:19.470 --> 00:26:22.533
And that let's just put in num vec.

00:26:24.457 --> 00:26:27.110
And there it is looking at num vec again,

00:26:27.110 --> 00:26:28.620
it takes each of these values.

00:26:28.620 --> 00:26:33.120
So for example, three,
three plus five is eight

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:35.540
times three is 24,

00:26:35.540 --> 00:26:38.727
6 plus five is 11 times
three is 33 and so on.

00:26:38.727 --> 00:26:40.480
And it returns to all those values.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:42.240
And of course, because
this was the only value

00:26:42.240 --> 00:26:43.073
in the function

00:26:43.073 --> 00:26:44.900
we could have also just
ignored that X equals

00:26:44.900 --> 00:26:46.030
and just written it like that

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:47.680
and we would get the same result.

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:54.210
Okay. Now let's move on to a
few miscellaneous functions.

00:26:54.270 --> 00:26:55.820
Actually, two of those functions,

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:58.030
we've already gone over up here,

00:26:58.030 --> 00:26:59.940
the installed dot packages function

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:02.090
that we use for installing
any new packages

00:27:02.090 --> 00:27:05.240
that our computer does not
currently have installed,

00:27:05.240 --> 00:27:06.640
but once they are installed,

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:08.680
you do not need to install them again.

00:27:08.680 --> 00:27:11.010
You only need to use the library function

00:27:11.010 --> 00:27:12.820
to load up those packages

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:15.610
whenever you have a new R session,

00:27:15.610 --> 00:27:17.960
I'm going to delete this
'cause this error right here,

00:27:17.960 --> 00:27:20.040
actually, I'm gonna comment it out.

00:27:20.040 --> 00:27:20.873
There we go. All right.

00:27:20.873 --> 00:27:24.670
So that's what the library function does.

00:27:24.670 --> 00:27:27.160
You always need to load a new package

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:29.820
into your R working space,

00:27:29.820 --> 00:27:33.130
into your new session as it's called

00:27:33.130 --> 00:27:35.440
whenever you are working with it again.

00:27:35.440 --> 00:27:37.610
If I reloaded R and didn't run this,

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:39.780
I would get an error if I
tried to run these functions,

00:27:39.780 --> 00:27:41.220
because it doesn't know where they are.

00:27:41.220 --> 00:27:45.210
You can think of it as
install dot packages

00:27:45.210 --> 00:27:46.960
is you going to Home Depot

00:27:46.960 --> 00:27:50.320
and buying a bunch of new,
really cool fancy tools

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:55.320
and then library is you
going into your shop

00:27:55.410 --> 00:27:58.230
and pulling out those tools
and getting them ready to use.

00:27:58.230 --> 00:28:00.423
Okay, there's a few other
miscellaneous functions here

00:28:00.423 --> 00:28:01.653
that are useful.

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:04.450
There is the help function.

00:28:04.450 --> 00:28:06.350
If you ever don't know
what a function does,

00:28:06.350 --> 00:28:09.320
just use the help
function on that function.

00:28:09.320 --> 00:28:11.990
So let's say, we want
to know what grepl does.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:15.460
All we have to do is help grepl, run that

00:28:15.460 --> 00:28:16.860
and it will automatically open

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:19.080
this little help window here to the right

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:22.540
where we can see all about grep and grepl

00:28:22.540 --> 00:28:24.410
and all these other
functions that are related

00:28:24.410 --> 00:28:26.110
and what they do and how they work,

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:28.010
the different arguments in them.

00:28:28.010 --> 00:28:29.010
And then the best part

00:28:29.010 --> 00:28:30.730
that I usually scroll down to immediately,

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:33.120
when I really wanna figure
out how a function works

00:28:33.120 --> 00:28:38.030
is the examples section where
you can see concrete examples

00:28:38.030 --> 00:28:40.740
in R for how the
particular functions work.

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:43.160
And you can just copy and
paste them right into here

00:28:43.160 --> 00:28:44.330
and see what happens.

00:28:44.330 --> 00:28:48.360
Another way to access
this help database here

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:51.150
is simply to go to help
in this panel here,

00:28:51.150 --> 00:28:54.170
type in the function you're looking for,

00:28:54.170 --> 00:28:56.320
hit enter, and it will come up on here.

00:28:56.320 --> 00:28:58.230
And then you can search within this page

00:28:58.230 --> 00:28:59.880
just by clicking here.

00:28:59.880 --> 00:29:02.510
Let's say, we want to look up just grepl,

00:29:02.510 --> 00:29:06.600
not grep or anything
else, we got it there.

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:07.640
You can keep scrolling through

00:29:07.640 --> 00:29:10.050
and see all the times that that word

00:29:10.050 --> 00:29:12.040
occurs in this help file.

00:29:12.040 --> 00:29:15.200
Then we have the data function.

00:29:15.200 --> 00:29:16.680
This is fun because

00:29:16.680 --> 00:29:19.170
we can access a whole
bunch of built-in data sets

00:29:19.170 --> 00:29:21.430
for playing around and experimenting in R

00:29:21.430 --> 00:29:22.490
as we are learning.

00:29:22.490 --> 00:29:26.300
If we run the data function
with empty parentheses,

00:29:26.300 --> 00:29:27.133
nothing in it,

00:29:28.010 --> 00:29:31.270
we get this list of all
these different data sets

00:29:31.270 --> 00:29:33.820
with a short little
description of what they are.

