on the survival of
passengers on the Titanic.

00:29:40.100 --> 00:29:41.380
Ooh. Okay.

00:29:41.380 --> 00:29:43.580
So notice that the data
set's called Titanic

00:29:43.580 --> 00:29:47.430
with a capital T, we can
go here type in Titanic

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:49.360
and run that.

00:29:49.360 --> 00:29:52.300
And now, if we look at our environment,

00:29:52.300 --> 00:29:56.750
we have this new data set here, Titanic,

00:29:56.750 --> 00:29:57.720
and then the values.

00:29:57.720 --> 00:29:59.530
For some reason, it says promise here.

00:29:59.530 --> 00:30:02.370
I have no idea what that means.

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:04.260
I guess, R is promising

00:30:04.260 --> 00:30:06.500
that they're gonna give us the data.

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:07.540
I don't know what that means.

00:30:07.540 --> 00:30:11.100
Either way, you can just click
on it and it's right there.

00:30:11.100 --> 00:30:13.820
Or if we had run it,

00:30:13.820 --> 00:30:16.670
then you could save it as its own file,

00:30:16.670 --> 00:30:19.553
My Titanic data.

00:30:23.130 --> 00:30:28.130
Doing that also turns it
from saying promise here

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:32.060
to an actual data set.

00:30:32.060 --> 00:30:33.293
So if we take a look at,

00:30:35.770 --> 00:30:38.980
oh, I guess it's like a weird,

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:42.040
this isn't a good dataset
example, but the point,

00:30:42.040 --> 00:30:45.250
'cause it has all these
different components to it.

00:30:45.250 --> 00:30:49.920
But anyway, the point is that
you run this data function

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:51.300
with the name of the data set,

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:52.710
and you can extract those data.

00:30:52.710 --> 00:30:56.490
You'll notice up here, that's
what I did with trees data.

00:30:56.490 --> 00:30:58.010
So I ran data trees,

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:02.206
saved trees as its own data
frame called the trees data.

00:31:02.206 --> 00:31:04.233
And that's this right here.

00:31:05.500 --> 00:31:07.120
And then I did a few modifications

00:31:07.120 --> 00:31:09.500
to add a few columns to it.

00:31:09.500 --> 00:31:12.380
I added this column of light

00:31:12.380 --> 00:31:15.320
and then use these functions
that I already went over

00:31:15.320 --> 00:31:20.320
rep and C to create this vector
of sun and shade treatments.

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:24.090
We run just that.

00:31:24.090 --> 00:31:26.380
You can see it's a
vector of sun and shade,

00:31:26.380 --> 00:31:28.980
and we're just adding
that into the tree dataset

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:31.880
and then making sun
and shade into a factor

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:33.313
using as dot factor.

00:31:35.200 --> 00:31:37.510
So if we take a look at tree data now,

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:38.830
it's the same as before.

00:31:38.830 --> 00:31:41.890
And we also have this extra
column of shade and sun values.

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:44.280
You can also learn more about the data

00:31:44.280 --> 00:31:48.560
that you've loaded in using
the data function by searching,

00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:49.560
by using the help function.

00:31:49.560 --> 00:31:51.280
So we'll put Titanic,

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:52.660
or actually let's put trees,

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:57.000
that's more ecological than Titanic data.

00:31:57.000 --> 00:32:00.240
We'll put trees into the help function.

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:03.220
And here we have this
really nice explanation

00:32:03.220 --> 00:32:05.230
for this tree's dataset,

00:32:05.230 --> 00:32:07.350
which explains what these
different columns mean,

00:32:07.350 --> 00:32:08.530
how they're measured

00:32:08.530 --> 00:32:11.210
and sources or references for
where these data came from.

00:32:11.210 --> 00:32:13.670
And a few examples of playing
around with these data.

00:32:13.670 --> 00:32:16.280
All right, so that's it for
