00:32:50.900 --> 00:32:54.980
Okay, now let's get on to
some basic data visualization.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.690
The most common and basic function

00:32:56.690 --> 00:32:58.870
that you'll ever wanna use
for plotting your data in R

00:32:58.870 --> 00:33:01.423
is just called plot.

00:33:02.720 --> 00:33:05.950
It's great for creating
scatterplots as well as box plots,

00:33:05.950 --> 00:33:07.010
as well as a few other things,

00:33:07.010 --> 00:33:09.470
but I'm gonna limit the
conversation to that

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.150
'cause those are probably
the most common plots

00:33:11.150 --> 00:33:12.940
you'll ever need to create.

00:33:12.940 --> 00:33:15.460
So let's use our tree data for this.

00:33:15.460 --> 00:33:17.730
So the plot function takes an argument,

00:33:17.730 --> 00:33:20.170
data equals tree data.

00:33:20.170 --> 00:33:22.280
Remember we were just taking
a look at that data set

00:33:22.280 --> 00:33:24.250
just before, let's take a
look at it one more time

00:33:24.250 --> 00:33:27.300
so I can just show you
what those variables are.

00:33:27.300 --> 00:33:30.520
Girth is the diameter of the tree.

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:32.090
Height is the height of the tree.

00:33:32.090 --> 00:33:34.290
Volume is the volume.

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:35.910
Girth is in inches.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.180
Height is in feet and volume
is in cubic feet, I believe.

00:33:39.180 --> 00:33:41.570
And this is this made
up additional data set

00:33:41.570 --> 00:33:44.380
of whether the tree was in
the sun or in the shade.

00:33:44.380 --> 00:33:46.930
So if we, so we'll use this data argument

00:33:46.930 --> 00:33:49.270
to tell the plot function
that we're gonna be using

00:33:49.270 --> 00:33:50.910
in the tree data.

00:33:50.910 --> 00:33:52.510
And then all we have to do

00:33:52.510 --> 00:33:57.510
is put in the names of the
columns that we want to plot.

00:33:58.580 --> 00:34:03.420
And we'll say height, and then
we'll use this tilde symbol,

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:05.833
which can be thought of as,

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:12.080
tilde equals is a function of,

00:34:12.080 --> 00:34:13.810
or more simply,

00:34:13.810 --> 00:34:16.320
you can just say whatever

00:34:17.780 --> 00:34:22.603
is on the left of the tilde, tilde,

00:34:23.500 --> 00:34:27.040
is the Y variable

00:34:32.180 --> 00:34:36.960
or it's what will appear

00:34:36.960 --> 00:34:38.933
on the Y axis.

00:34:41.960 --> 00:34:45.760
So here let's have tree
height on the Y axis

00:34:45.760 --> 00:34:49.410
and let's do tree volume

00:34:51.950 --> 00:34:53.710
on the x-axis.

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:55.610
And when we run this,

00:34:55.610 --> 00:35:00.050
we get a nice little plot
of height on the y-axis,

00:35:00.050 --> 00:35:03.540
volume on the x-axis, a nice
little simple scatter plot.

00:35:03.540 --> 00:35:05.880
We can also create a box plot

00:35:05.880 --> 00:35:09.110
if the X variable in this case volume

00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:10.713
is a categorical variable.

00:35:11.880 --> 00:35:13.450
So let's keep height there.

00:35:13.450 --> 00:35:16.780
But instead of volume, let's
look at sun versus shade.

00:35:16.780 --> 00:35:19.550
I think we called that column light.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:20.740
If we run that,

00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:24.260
now we get a simple box
plot with sun and shade,

00:35:24.260 --> 00:35:29.260
and the box plots created
around those values of height.

00:35:29.620 --> 00:35:31.560
The next most essential plotting function

00:35:31.560 --> 00:35:35.250
is the hist function
or histogram function.

00:35:35.250 --> 00:35:36.590
There's so many times that you want

00:35:36.590 --> 00:35:38.850
to just quickly visualize
what your data looked like,

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:40.420
how are they distributed?

00:35:40.420 --> 00:35:42.220
What kind of data are you working with?

00:35:42.220 --> 00:35:43.500
You just collected those data.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:45.710
You just came back from
the field, enter them all.

00:35:45.710 --> 00:35:47.150
What's the first thing
you're gonna look at?

00:35:47.150 --> 00:35:50.610
Gosh, do my data look
reasonable or are they crazy?

00:35:50.610 --> 00:35:52.320
Are there some crazy outliers out there?

00:35:52.320 --> 00:35:55.450
Let's take a quick look
using the hist function.

00:35:55.450 --> 00:35:58.753
Let's just use tree data,

00:36:00.520 --> 00:36:02.453
dollar sign height.

00:36:03.340 --> 00:36:05.200
That's a capital H I guess.

00:36:05.200 --> 00:36:08.100
Let's look at a
distribution of tree heights

00:36:08.100 --> 00:36:11.190
in our data set and there it is.

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:12.023
Okay.

00:36:12.023 --> 00:36:14.330
Finally, sometimes when we create plots,

00:36:14.330 --> 00:36:16.560
we also want to add lines to them.

00:36:16.560 --> 00:36:20.680
Let's take a look at this
scatterplot that we had up here.

00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:22.220
Plot that one more time,

00:36:22.220 --> 00:36:25.920
let's say that we want
to add a line to this,

00:36:25.920 --> 00:36:28.210
a best fit line that
shows the relationship

00:36:28.210 --> 00:36:29.840
between tree height and tree volume.

00:36:29.840 --> 00:36:31.720
Let's say it's a linear relationship

00:36:31.720 --> 00:36:33.210
for the sake of simplicity.

00:36:33.210 --> 00:36:34.560
Let's create a quick model,

00:36:34.560 --> 00:36:36.750
this kind of gets a
little bit more advanced

00:36:36.750 --> 00:36:39.400
on the essential functions
of our cheat sheet.

00:36:39.400 --> 00:36:40.780
We'll go back to this later,

00:36:40.780 --> 00:36:42.980
but let's create a simple model.

00:36:42.980 --> 00:36:47.980
We'll call it fitted model
and we'll use the LM function

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:50.800
which stands for linear model.

00:36:50.800 --> 00:36:53.140
And this actually takes the same syntax

00:36:53.140 --> 00:36:54.563
as the plot function.

00:36:56.810 --> 00:36:58.240
Height is a function of volume

00:36:58.240 --> 00:37:00.530
and the data are the tree data.

00:37:00.530 --> 00:37:02.850
This is going to be our fitted model

00:37:02.850 --> 00:37:05.560
and then we're gonna plot these data.

00:37:05.560 --> 00:37:07.963
I'm just rearranging the order here.

00:37:08.910 --> 00:37:11.170
We're gonna plot this scatter plot,

00:37:11.170 --> 00:37:16.170
and then we're going to
use the function abline.

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:17.630
Now remember it with a linear model,

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:21.170
you've got an A coefficient
and a B coefficient.

00:37:21.170 --> 00:37:22.633
So for a linear model,

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:25.540
going back to some basic algebra,

00:37:25.540 --> 00:37:29.290
Y equals A plus B X,

00:37:29.290 --> 00:37:31.340
that is the formula for a linear model.

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:34.950
An AB line means, give
us an A give us a B,

00:37:34.950 --> 00:37:37.033
and we will create a line for you.

00:37:38.350 --> 00:37:42.243
So the nice thing is that when
we look at this fitted model,

00:37:43.705 --> 00:37:45.680
the fitted model returns
these two coefficients,

00:37:45.680 --> 00:37:47.460
the intercept, which is the A

00:37:47.460 --> 00:37:51.833
and the slope of the volume, which is B.

00:37:52.740 --> 00:37:55.240
So if we enter that fitted model

00:37:55.240 --> 00:37:57.600
right inside this AB line function

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.423
right after we plotted the data,

00:38:00.970 --> 00:38:03.603
we have this line that
is thrown right on top.

00:38:04.540 --> 00:38:07.080
But sometimes you don't just
want a nice fitted line,

00:38:07.080 --> 00:38:10.080
you want to mark certain
thresholds in the data,

00:38:10.080 --> 00:38:12.510
but using vertical or horizontal lines.

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:16.110
To do that, still use the abline function,

00:38:16.110 --> 00:38:19.190
but we can use V for vertical equals,

00:38:19.190 --> 00:38:24.010
so let's say we want a vertical
line at a volume of 40.

00:38:24.010 --> 00:38:27.080
So we'll say ABline V equals 40.

00:38:27.080 --> 00:38:30.520
And there we have a vertical
line at volume equals 40,

00:38:30.520 --> 00:38:35.520
or maybe we'll do ABline
H for horizontal at

00:38:37.470 --> 00:38:39.063
height equals 85.

00:38:41.710 --> 00:38:42.940
And there we have it.

00:38:42.940 --> 00:38:47.063
Now let's move on to
working with data frames.

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:55.710
I just created the stall heading here.

00:38:55.710 --> 00:38:58.500
Didn't create it for the other
sections. It doesn't matter.

00:38:58.500 --> 00:39:01.400
Let's just start working
with data frames now.

00:39:01.400 --> 00:39:04.010
I'm never really sure if data
frames should be one word

00:39:04.010 --> 00:39:05.013
or two words,

00:39:05.900 --> 00:39:07.780
Word and other applications always tell me

00:39:07.780 --> 00:39:09.490
that I spelled data frames wrong.

00:39:09.490 --> 00:39:11.270
Wow, look, our studio is telling me

00:39:11.270 --> 00:39:13.640
that I spelled data frames
wrong by having them together,

00:39:13.640 --> 00:39:17.320
then maybe it is really
just data space frames.

00:39:17.320 --> 00:39:18.546
All right.

00:39:18.546 --> 00:39:19.960
So the most common function

00:39:19.960 --> 00:39:22.070
that I've used when
working with data frames

00:39:22.070 --> 00:39:25.150
is just the names function.

00:39:25.150 --> 00:39:28.340
And what this does is it
quickly allows us to see

00:39:28.340 --> 00:39:31.280
the names of all the
columns in our data frame.

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:35.963
So if we use our original data frame here,

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:39.270
you can see that one
column is called num vec.

00:39:39.270 --> 00:39:40.980
Another is called species vec.

00:39:40.980 --> 00:39:45.980
If we use the names
function for our tree data,

00:39:47.140 --> 00:39:50.347
we have girth, height, volume, and light,

00:39:50.347 --> 00:39:51.900
and we can also use the names function

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:53.920
to rename column headings.

00:39:53.920 --> 00:39:58.920
So for example, let's index
out just the column of girth.

00:40:00.160 --> 00:40:02.360
So that's just the first value of names.

00:40:02.360 --> 00:40:04.100
So if we run that, we get just girth

00:40:04.100 --> 00:40:08.240
and into that, we're
going to rename it as DBH,

00:40:08.240 --> 00:40:09.790
which stands for diameter
at breast height,

00:40:09.790 --> 00:40:13.230
which is a fancier way of
saying girth and run that.

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:15.333
And now we take a look at tree data again,

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:19.760
and the first column
name has been renamed.

00:40:19.760 --> 00:40:21.650
And if we take a look at the names

00:40:21.650 --> 00:40:23.690
for our tree data one more
time by just selecting that

00:40:23.690 --> 00:40:24.890
and running that alone,

00:40:24.890 --> 00:40:27.170
DBH, height, volume, and light.

00:40:27.170 --> 00:40:30.620
I often use it for quickly
looking at the names

00:40:30.620 --> 00:40:33.090
of a data frame when I'm trying to refer

00:40:33.090 --> 00:40:34.730
or index certain parts of a data frame

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.190
and I just forgot what
that column was called.

00:40:37.190 --> 00:40:40.670
Next we have the data dot frame function,

00:40:40.670 --> 00:40:43.240
which is what we use to
create simple data frames

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:44.310
when we have a bunch of vectors

00:40:44.310 --> 00:40:46.297
that we want to bring
together into one data frame.

00:40:46.297 --> 00:40:48.490
And if you recall up here,

00:40:48.490 --> 00:40:53.180
that's what we did with the
two vectors that we created

00:40:53.180 --> 00:40:54.800
when we started

00:40:54.800 --> 00:40:58.940
num vec, species vec and
then we use data dot frame

00:40:58.940 --> 00:41:02.720
to combine these two
vectors into one data frame.

00:41:02.720 --> 00:41:04.280
Now note that both of these vectors

00:41:04.280 --> 00:41:07.700
need to be the same
length with one exception,

00:41:07.700 --> 00:41:11.140
you can have vectors
that are a length of one.

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:14.047
So for example, let's create a new column

00:41:14.047 --> 00:41:15.473
and we'll call it,

00:41:16.510 --> 00:41:18.940
My new column.

00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:20.870
Cause I'm that original,

00:41:20.870 --> 00:41:21.790
equals

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:24.400
let's just say my new column,

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.150
we would just want the value
of two inside my new column.

00:41:27.150 --> 00:41:28.721
Why not?

00:41:28.721 --> 00:41:31.550
When we run that and look at data frame,

00:41:31.550 --> 00:41:34.380
we have this new column of twos.

00:41:34.380 --> 00:41:37.090
And the reason that worked is because

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:39.820
two in this case is one element.

00:41:39.820 --> 00:41:43.900
It's the same as a vector
of just this one element

00:41:43.900 --> 00:41:47.450
is evenly divisible into
the number of rows here.

00:41:47.450 --> 00:41:50.860
So we get two divided into,

00:41:50.860 --> 00:41:52.180
I'm not making any sense.

00:41:52.180 --> 00:41:54.790
The point is that two is a length of one

00:41:54.790 --> 00:41:58.670
and you can easily fit the two into each.

00:41:58.670 --> 00:41:59.503
Okay. You know what?

00:41:59.503 --> 00:42:02.160
I can't explain this. Let
me show you another example.

00:42:02.160 --> 00:42:05.660
If we had said one comma two,

00:42:05.660 --> 00:42:08.170
so now we have a vector
of the value one and two

00:42:08.170 --> 00:42:09.770
and run that, it will still work

00:42:10.750 --> 00:42:13.070
because now it'll repeat the one and two

00:42:13.070 --> 00:42:14.910
throughout this entire data frame.

00:42:14.910 --> 00:42:19.260
However, if we had three
values, one, two, and four,

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:22.640
that won't evenly fit
within the available space

00:42:22.640 --> 00:42:23.473
of this data frame.

00:42:23.473 --> 00:42:25.520
And we'll get an error,

00:42:25.520 --> 00:42:27.320
error in data frame,

00:42:27.320 --> 00:42:31.160
arguments implied
differing number of rows.

00:42:31.160 --> 00:42:32.240
There it is.

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:35.450
So, it usually never have to do this,

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.150
but just letting you know
that that's an option.

00:42:38.150 --> 00:42:39.610
Maybe I shouldn't have told you.

00:42:39.610 --> 00:42:40.800
Next we have,

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:42.210
actually speaking of telling people

00:42:42.210 --> 00:42:43.710
more than they need to know,

00:42:43.710 --> 00:42:46.350
I'm probably gonna remove data dot frame

00:42:46.350 --> 00:42:48.970
from future cheat sheets

00:42:48.970 --> 00:42:52.050
from the future versions of
this essential of R cheat sheet,

00:42:52.050 --> 00:42:54.470
because there is a far superior way

00:42:54.470 --> 00:42:58.120
to make data frames in R,
and those are called tibbles.

00:42:58.120 --> 00:43:01.920
So instead of data frame, data dot frame,

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:06.920
let's create our first my
tibble, again, very original.

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:08.820
And here, instead of data frame,

00:43:08.820 --> 00:43:11.530
we're going to use the function tibble,

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:14.560
which does the exact same
thing that data dot frame does

00:43:14.560 --> 00:43:15.770
creating a data frame,

00:43:15.770 --> 00:43:19.760
except the data frame is now
really cool and superior,

00:43:19.760 --> 00:43:21.390
and it's not called a data frame anymore,

00:43:21.390 --> 00:43:22.620
it's called a Tibble,

00:43:22.620 --> 00:43:25.477
or maybe you call it a type of data frame,

00:43:25.477 --> 00:43:26.810
Tibble type of data frame.

00:43:26.810 --> 00:43:27.643
I don't know.

00:43:27.643 --> 00:43:32.020
Anyway, it stands for tidy
table when we run that,

00:43:32.020 --> 00:43:34.780
oh, we can't find the function Tibble.

00:43:34.780 --> 00:43:37.680
And that's because we have to load up

00:43:37.680 --> 00:43:42.033
the package dplyr first
to work with Tibbles.

00:43:43.890 --> 00:43:46.180
When we run that, and again,

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:49.180
you can install the package
if you don't have dplyr first.

00:43:49.180 --> 00:43:50.820
It stands for data pliers.

00:43:50.820 --> 00:43:54.530
So remember you use pliers
for bending and manipulating

00:43:54.530 --> 00:43:57.190
and changing things and wrangling things.

00:43:57.190 --> 00:44:00.100
And so that's what dplyr is, but for data,

00:44:00.100 --> 00:44:02.870
now, we should be able to run this

00:44:02.870 --> 00:44:05.167
and take a look at my tibble.

00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:09.150
And there it is, we have,

00:44:09.150 --> 00:44:11.450
up here it clearly tells
us that it's a tibble.

00:44:11.450 --> 00:44:12.990
So thank you very much for that.

00:44:12.990 --> 00:44:17.920
It shows us that it is four
rows long by two columns wide.

00:44:17.920 --> 00:44:20.580
It shows us the type of data

00:44:20.580 --> 00:44:23.190
that each of these columns comprises of.

00:44:23.190 --> 00:44:28.190
So here num vec is a
double or numeric column,

00:44:28.190 --> 00:44:31.480
species vec is a character column.

00:44:31.480 --> 00:44:35.560
And yeah, so it includes all
of this neat information here,

00:44:35.560 --> 00:44:37.350
but actually the coolest
thing about Tibbles

00:44:37.350 --> 00:44:39.830
is not something we can see
from this really simple dataset.

00:44:39.830 --> 00:44:44.063
So let's create a tibble
using our tree data.

00:44:50.120 --> 00:44:52.170
My tree tibble. That's what I'll call it.

00:44:53.080 --> 00:44:57.210
Now, taking a look at my tree Tibble,

00:44:57.210 --> 00:44:59.670
here's the really cool
thing down here it says

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:02.320
with 21 more rows.

00:45:02.320 --> 00:45:04.840
Now what's really nice about this

00:45:04.840 --> 00:45:09.240
is that if we had just looked
at tree data not as a Tibble,

00:45:09.240 --> 00:45:12.233
so the original version of
tree data is not as a Tibble.

00:45:13.570 --> 00:45:15.100
Here are the data.

00:45:15.100 --> 00:45:17.100
Oh, wait, what are the column names?

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:19.260
We have to scroll up a
bunch to get to here.

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:21.500
Oh, okay. Wow. That's a lot of space.

00:45:21.500 --> 00:45:23.390
I don't wanna have to like open this all.

00:45:23.390 --> 00:45:24.290
Oh, wait, what happened?

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:27.250
Oh, now I have to, how
do I make, oh, I have,

00:45:27.250 --> 00:45:30.070
oh no, there we go, okay.

00:45:30.070 --> 00:45:31.890
Yeah, you get my point.

00:45:31.890 --> 00:45:33.480
Tibbles are really cool.

00:45:33.480 --> 00:45:35.280
There's a bunch of other
advantages to them too,

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:37.670
but for now, I'm just gonna say

00:45:37.670 --> 00:45:40.770
they're a neat and simple way to look at

00:45:40.770 --> 00:45:42.290
and work with data frames.

00:45:42.290 --> 00:45:47.270
So now moving on to the next
function as dot data dot frame,

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:50.760
sometimes you end up working with matrices

00:45:50.760 --> 00:45:53.280
and that you want to
convert to a data frame.

00:45:53.280 --> 00:45:55.170
So for example,

00:45:55.170 --> 00:45:56.003
up here again,

00:45:56.003 --> 00:45:57.010
we have,

00:45:57.010 --> 00:45:59.450
we use the as dot matrix to convert,

00:45:59.450 --> 00:46:01.450
so I'm kind of just going backwards now.

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:04.090
We can take our original data frame,

00:46:04.090 --> 00:46:07.230
convert it to a matrix
using as dot matrix.

00:46:07.230 --> 00:46:08.240
Don't need to know that function.

00:46:08.240 --> 00:46:09.690
I'm just kind of showing you,

00:46:10.590 --> 00:46:12.360
trying to create a matrix
here to show you what I mean.

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.170
So here we have this
simple matrix of values.

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:16.920
Usually you end up working with matrices

00:46:16.920 --> 00:46:20.210
when you're creating
species by site matrices

00:46:20.210 --> 00:46:25.210
for multi-variate ordinations
in ecology and community,

00:46:25.700 --> 00:46:28.130
when you're dealing with
community data in ecology

00:46:28.130 --> 00:46:28.963
and that kind of thing.

00:46:28.963 --> 00:46:32.810
So that's usually where you'll
encounter matrices the most.

00:46:32.810 --> 00:46:34.990
So let's say we have this matrix here

00:46:34.990 --> 00:46:37.250
and we want to convert it to a data frame

00:46:37.250 --> 00:46:42.250
use as dot data frame with my matrix.

00:46:42.610 --> 00:46:46.350
And there it is back to a data frame

00:46:46.350 --> 00:46:51.350
and along the same lines we
also have as underscore Tibble.

00:46:52.150 --> 00:46:54.620
And the reason that most of the,

00:46:54.620 --> 00:46:57.870
so most of the functions
that come with dplyr

00:46:57.870 --> 00:47:01.640
have kind of done away
with using dots or periods

00:47:01.640 --> 00:47:04.740
to separate out words in functions

00:47:04.740 --> 00:47:06.320
and instead use underscores.

00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:08.390
And the reason for that is that

00:47:08.390 --> 00:47:10.800
if you're a Python programmer,

00:47:10.800 --> 00:47:14.510
the dots actually are
read in a different way

00:47:14.510 --> 00:47:15.800
than they are in R.

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:17.330
Where in R they just kind of

00:47:17.330 --> 00:47:18.800
become part of the name of the function

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:22.740
whereas in Python, they
actually are an operator

00:47:22.740 --> 00:47:25.710
that indicate something a little bit more

00:47:25.710 --> 00:47:27.830
than just part of the
name of the function.

00:47:27.830 --> 00:47:30.150
And so for that reason to
kind of simplify things

00:47:30.150 --> 00:47:32.970
and make them kind of
universally understandable

00:47:32.970 --> 00:47:35.580
between the two languages,
that's my understanding.

00:47:35.580 --> 00:47:38.870
Maybe someone knows or understands
a better reason for why,

00:47:38.870 --> 00:47:40.570
why they've done away with the periods.

00:47:40.570 --> 00:47:42.090
So instead of as dot Tibble here

00:47:42.090 --> 00:47:46.250
we have as underscore
tibble for this function.

00:47:46.250 --> 00:47:48.460
And what this does is it
actually does the same thing as

00:47:48.460 --> 00:47:51.180
as dot data frame, as dot data dot frame,

00:47:51.180 --> 00:47:53.750
but converts things into a tibble.

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:56.860
So actually up here, we probably should,

00:47:56.860 --> 00:47:58.930
instead of using Tibble tree data,

00:47:58.930 --> 00:48:01.710
we really should have use as Tibble

00:48:01.710 --> 00:48:04.180
'cause that can actually
give you some errors.

00:48:04.180 --> 00:48:09.180
But if we use as Tibble,
let's take a look at that.

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:11.747
Here is our tree data tibble.

00:48:13.200 --> 00:48:17.700
Okay, now onto as dot matrix,

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:19.310
oh, I guess I am gonna go over that, yep.

00:48:19.310 --> 00:48:20.680
So I already did.

00:48:20.680 --> 00:48:24.210
As dot matrix is useful when
you want to create species

00:48:24.210 --> 00:48:27.600
by site matrices and
some functions require

00:48:27.600 --> 00:48:31.870
that you enter matrices into
their arguments rather than,

00:48:31.870 --> 00:48:33.130
than data frames.

00:48:33.130 --> 00:48:35.743
And so that's what you
would use as dot matrix for.

00:48:38.110 --> 00:48:41.570
So again, here is the matrix of values

00:48:41.570 --> 00:48:44.480
based on this original
data frame that we had.

00:48:44.480 --> 00:48:48.110
So with matrices, sometimes
you need to swap them around.

00:48:48.110 --> 00:48:50.440
So swap what is in the column,

00:48:50.440 --> 00:48:53.222
make the columns into rows and
make the rows into columns.

00:48:53.222 --> 00:48:57.340
And we can do that with the T function.

00:48:57.340 --> 00:48:59.983
Or I think that stands for transpose.

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:02.333
Let's see.

00:49:05.000 --> 00:49:06.730
T, matrix transpose.

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:08.330
Yes. Okay. I was right.

00:49:08.330 --> 00:49:09.163
All right.

00:49:09.163 --> 00:49:12.563
So when we run this function on my matrix,

00:49:13.650 --> 00:49:15.470
here we have that same matrix,

00:49:15.470 --> 00:49:18.890
but the columns and
rows have been reversed.

00:49:18.890 --> 00:49:22.657
Then we have the ncol
and the nrow functions,

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:27.640
which is just a quick, useful way

00:49:27.640 --> 00:49:29.960
to get how many columns or
rows we have in a data set.

00:49:29.960 --> 00:49:32.223
So let's look at our tree data, ncol.

00:49:33.536 --> 00:49:35.954
It looks like we have four
columns in the tree data.

00:49:35.954 --> 00:49:39.140
Nrow, looks like we have
31 rows in the tree data.

00:49:39.140 --> 00:49:40.760
Simple as that, nothing more to it.

00:49:40.760 --> 00:49:43.030
So I did mention the tibble

00:49:43.030 --> 00:49:45.360
as being quite superior to the data frame

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:47.160
because in what, well,

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:49.340
one of the most useful things is that it

00:49:49.340 --> 00:49:51.160
nicely and neatly cuts
off your data frames

00:49:51.160 --> 00:49:54.703
so that you can just see
the top 10 entries I guess.

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:57.860
You can also change what that value is.

00:49:58.710 --> 00:50:00.410
But either way you see the top few entries

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:02.810
instead of having to scroll
through the whole data set.

00:50:02.810 --> 00:50:04.760
But another way to take a look at that

00:50:04.760 --> 00:50:07.260
using just typical normal data frame

00:50:07.260 --> 00:50:09.063
is using the head function.

00:50:09.970 --> 00:50:13.770
And that just returns the top
few entries of a data frame.

00:50:13.770 --> 00:50:16.943
So if we'd look at tree data and use head,

00:50:17.890 --> 00:50:20.620
I think the default is
six, the top six rows.

00:50:20.620 --> 00:50:24.490
So here we see the top
six rows of the tree data.

00:50:24.490 --> 00:50:27.860
The reason this isn't as
great as using a tibble

00:50:27.860 --> 00:50:30.960
to begin with is it can
be a little bit misleading

00:50:30.960 --> 00:50:32.980
if you didn't realize that
you ran the head function.

00:50:32.980 --> 00:50:35.110
Cause it doesn't tell you
that there's more rows

00:50:35.110 --> 00:50:36.960
in this data frame that are missing.

00:50:36.960 --> 00:50:38.700
It's just a quick way to extract

00:50:38.700 --> 00:50:40.313
the top little bit of a dataset.

00:50:41.230 --> 00:50:45.410
Okay, now I'm going to talk
about one more function

00:50:45.410 --> 00:50:47.390
that I use tremendously.

00:50:47.390 --> 00:50:49.380
I love it. It's very useful.

00:50:49.380 --> 00:50:50.827
There's a lot of ways to use it

00:50:50.827 --> 00:50:55.173
and that is the left join function.

00:50:56.270 --> 00:50:58.160
Now note, there's also a right join

00:50:58.160 --> 00:50:59.450
and a few other types of joins,

00:50:59.450 --> 00:51:01.580
which you can learn about
by searching them here.

00:51:01.580 --> 00:51:02.870
But I'm just gonna talk about left join,

00:51:02.870 --> 00:51:05.150
and then you can figure
out the rest after that.

00:51:05.150 --> 00:51:09.460
So what this does is it is
used to combine two data frames

00:51:09.460 --> 00:51:11.460
based on a reference column.

00:51:11.460 --> 00:51:14.423
And this again also
uses the dplyr package.

00:51:18.170 --> 00:51:21.713
So make sure to install and
load that library first.

00:51:22.779 --> 00:51:24.310
So let's say,

00:51:24.310 --> 00:51:27.830
let's create two data
frames so we can actually

00:51:27.830 --> 00:51:29.433
show how this works.

00:51:30.330 --> 00:51:33.290
Let's see, let's take a look at tree data.

00:51:33.290 --> 00:51:35.260
Let's use it as a tibble.

00:51:35.260 --> 00:51:37.970
Let's use the My tree Tibble

00:51:37.970 --> 00:51:40.460
since I keep talking about
how great Tibbles are.

00:51:40.460 --> 00:51:41.940
We take a look at the tree data.

00:51:41.940 --> 00:51:46.940
Okay, let's add a column
that indicates the,

00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:49.050
the identity of the tree.

00:51:49.050 --> 00:51:50.740
So which tree we're dealing with.

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:54.403
We'll just do that by saying tree ID.

00:51:55.640 --> 00:51:57.780
And I think there's 31 rows total. Yeah.

00:51:57.780 --> 00:52:01.360
So here we have 10 rows with
21 more. So that's 31 rows.

00:52:01.360 --> 00:52:02.490
So into tree ID,

00:52:02.490 --> 00:52:06.890
we're just gonna say add in
the values of one through 31

00:52:06.890 --> 00:52:09.550
and we'll make this as dot character

00:52:11.980 --> 00:52:14.040
just so that it's clear

00:52:14.040 --> 00:52:16.020
that these numbers don't
mean anything as a number,

00:52:16.020 --> 00:52:18.600
but they're just the
identity of each tree.

00:52:18.600 --> 00:52:20.737
Let's take a look at my tree Tibble.

00:52:22.430 --> 00:52:26.580
So here we have this new
column now called tree ID.

00:52:26.580 --> 00:52:28.310
Okay, and let's say we have,

00:52:28.310 --> 00:52:30.253
let's make a new data frame now.

00:52:31.200 --> 00:52:32.680
Tree ID

00:52:35.350 --> 00:52:36.743
1 through 31.

00:52:39.840 --> 00:52:42.700
Let's create it with the Tibble function.

00:52:42.700 --> 00:52:46.480
So tree ID is 1 through
31, and then let's see,

00:52:46.480 --> 00:52:48.320
let's make up some data.

00:52:48.320 --> 00:52:50.780
Tree leaves.

00:52:50.780 --> 00:52:53.440
Let's just say we could count
how many leaves each tree has.

00:52:53.440 --> 00:52:55.410
I dunno, I'm just making this up again.

00:52:55.410 --> 00:52:59.015
Let's just say tree leaves equals let's,

00:52:59.015 --> 00:53:00.510
I'm gonna use a few extra functions here

00:53:00.510 --> 00:53:01.520
just to make this easy.

00:53:01.520 --> 00:53:05.200
So I'll, actually, let's do run if.

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:08.060
So that's picking a random number

00:53:08.060 --> 00:53:09.940
from a uniform distribution.

00:53:09.940 --> 00:53:13.610
When we type it in, we get
this little window that pops up

00:53:13.610 --> 00:53:16.210
that shows us what we can enter in it.

00:53:16.210 --> 00:53:18.330
Now I'm not getting that to pop up again.

00:53:18.330 --> 00:53:19.740
There we go.

00:53:19.740 --> 00:53:21.760
N is the number of values min and max,

00:53:21.760 --> 00:53:25.840
so we'll say, N equals 31,

00:53:25.840 --> 00:53:29.730
mean equals 3,000,

00:53:29.730 --> 00:53:32.990
max equals 5,000 leaves.

00:53:32.990 --> 00:53:35.390
Let's say and the problem
is that these values,

00:53:35.390 --> 00:53:40.070
if we ran just that, they
give us decimals as well.

00:53:40.070 --> 00:53:42.590
So let's just round that to whole numbers.

00:53:42.590 --> 00:53:45.620
Since let's just say we can
only count entire leaves

00:53:45.620 --> 00:53:47.640
and not partial parts of leaves.

00:53:47.640 --> 00:53:49.393
So round,

00:53:50.810 --> 00:53:51.910
that should be enough.

00:53:53.190 --> 00:53:54.023
Run that,

00:53:54.023 --> 00:53:56.240
taking a look at this one more time.

00:53:56.240 --> 00:53:57.073
Yup. There we go.

00:53:57.073 --> 00:53:59.730
We have these 31 different leaf counts

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:00.920
for these different trees

00:54:00.920 --> 00:54:03.743
and we're adding that
as column tree leaves.

00:54:05.930 --> 00:54:10.440
So now let's save that as My tree

00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:13.873
leaf data.

00:54:16.380 --> 00:54:17.610
Taking a look at this,

00:54:17.610 --> 00:54:20.320
we have this data set with the tree ID

00:54:20.320 --> 00:54:22.880
and the number of leaves for each tree.

00:54:22.880 --> 00:54:25.420
And then we have this other data set

00:54:25.420 --> 00:54:27.860
that has these other
variables and tree ID,

00:54:27.860 --> 00:54:30.210
but doesn't have the leaf data.

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:34.793
So we combine these together
with the left join function.

00:54:35.740 --> 00:54:39.850
So typically the first data
set that goes on the left

00:54:39.850 --> 00:54:43.200
is the one that has almost
everything already in it.

00:54:43.200 --> 00:54:46.540
So here we'll put in my tree Tibble

00:54:46.540 --> 00:54:48.400
and then the next data set

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:50.100
is the data set that contains the data

00:54:50.100 --> 00:54:54.360
we want to add to it, My tree leaf data.

00:54:54.360 --> 00:54:57.380
And then we add in one
more argument that says,

00:54:57.380 --> 00:55:01.110
by equals tree ID.

00:55:01.110 --> 00:55:02.510
This tells the function,

00:55:02.510 --> 00:55:04.790
let's take these data
and then take these data

00:55:04.790 --> 00:55:08.020
and add them to these data and match it up

00:55:08.020 --> 00:55:10.250
so that every value in this data set

00:55:10.250 --> 00:55:11.700
is matched up to this data set

00:55:11.700 --> 00:55:14.570
based on a matching tree ID value.

00:55:14.570 --> 00:55:16.180
In this case, it's actually the same order

00:55:16.180 --> 00:55:18.390
because tree IDs are in the same order.

00:55:18.390 --> 00:55:22.083
So let's mix up that order
actually to prove my point.

00:55:26.060 --> 00:55:28.100
So I'm just going to index this

00:55:29.668 --> 00:55:31.068
and I'm just going to sample

00:55:32.890 --> 00:55:36.020
31 values from one through 31

00:55:36.020 --> 00:55:38.570
that gives us a random order to everything

00:55:40.520 --> 00:55:41.630
and let's save

00:55:43.530 --> 00:55:45.530
that into itself.

00:55:45.530 --> 00:55:46.930
So now if we take a tree leaf,

00:55:46.930 --> 00:55:49.070
we take a look at tree leaf data.

00:55:49.070 --> 00:55:51.410
Now all the tree IDs are all mixed up,

00:55:51.410 --> 00:55:52.660
but they're still associated

00:55:52.660 --> 00:55:55.090
with each particular
number of tree leaves.

00:55:55.090 --> 00:55:56.550
So now if we do left join

00:55:56.550 --> 00:55:59.933
and let's save this as my data all,

00:56:03.480 --> 00:56:04.840
now, if we run left join,

00:56:04.840 --> 00:56:07.553
it should connect these
two data sets by tree ID.

00:56:10.190 --> 00:56:13.960
Error, can't join because
of incompatible types.

00:56:13.960 --> 00:56:15.770
Ah, I know why,

00:56:15.770 --> 00:56:18.750
because up here we saved
it as dot character,

00:56:18.750 --> 00:56:20.170
but here we did not.

00:56:20.170 --> 00:56:23.410
So let's save as dot character,

00:56:23.410 --> 00:56:25.520
it's trying to combine
numbers with letters

00:56:25.520 --> 00:56:26.930
and that doesn't work,

00:56:26.930 --> 00:56:29.190
even though they're both numbers
one of them is a character,

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:30.640
so it doesn't know what to do with that.

00:56:30.640 --> 00:56:31.793
Let's do that again.

00:56:32.980 --> 00:56:34.720
There we go. No error now.

00:56:34.720 --> 00:56:39.720
Let's take a look at my
data all and there it is.

00:56:40.480 --> 00:56:42.350
Now we have these tree leaves

00:56:42.350 --> 00:56:44.640
attached to this other dataset.

00:56:44.640 --> 00:56:47.980
So if we let, let's take
a look at tree ID one.

00:56:47.980 --> 00:56:51.240
Tree ID one is 4567,
oh, that's convenient.

00:56:51.240 --> 00:56:52.320
That's easy to remember.

00:56:52.320 --> 00:56:53.670
4567.

00:56:53.670 --> 00:56:58.670
Let's take a look at the
original My tree leaf data.

00:57:02.060 --> 00:57:03.510
Oh, look, it's in the top 10.

00:57:04.470 --> 00:57:07.040
Number one is 4567.

00:57:07.040 --> 00:57:09.690
And so it was able to successfully combine

00:57:09.690 --> 00:57:11.060
those two data sets.

00:57:11.060 --> 00:57:12.690
Now note that if we didn't include

00:57:12.690 --> 00:57:16.583
this by equals tree ID in
here, R is pretty smart.

00:57:17.670 --> 00:57:18.520
When we run that,

00:57:18.520 --> 00:57:21.800
it shows us joining by equals tree ID

00:57:21.800 --> 00:57:23.830
'cause it found that
that is the only column

00:57:23.830 --> 00:57:26.100
that's in common between
the two data sets.

00:57:26.100 --> 00:57:27.810
If there wasn't a column in common

00:57:27.810 --> 00:57:29.380
between the two data sets,

00:57:29.380 --> 00:57:31.660
we'd get an error if we did that.

00:57:31.660 --> 00:57:36.330
All right, now, let's get onto
the basics of data wrangling.

00:57:36.330 --> 00:57:38.810
This is where it gets really
fun and a little more advanced,

00:57:38.810 --> 00:57:41.110
but you can do some
really cool things here.

00:57:41.110 --> 00:57:44.590
All of these functions are
going to use the dplyr package.

00:57:44.590 --> 00:57:46.110
So if you haven't installed

00:57:46.110 --> 00:57:50.760
and run the library function
for dplyr, do so now.

00:57:50.760 --> 00:57:53.270
And the first first
function we're gonna go over

00:57:53.270 --> 00:57:55.440
is the select function.

00:57:55.440 --> 00:57:57.930
And this is simply used for filtering

00:57:57.930 --> 00:58:01.100
and renaming columns that you want to keep

00:58:01.100 --> 00:58:03.660
or remove from a data frame.

00:58:03.660 --> 00:58:07.163
Going back to let's use this
new data set that we created

00:58:07.163 --> 00:58:08.993
My data all.

00:58:10.150 --> 00:58:11.770
So the first argument of these functions

00:58:11.770 --> 00:58:14.400
is always the data set
that you want to use.

00:58:14.400 --> 00:58:16.020
So in this case, we'll use

00:58:16.020 --> 00:58:18.350
the My data all that we created just now,

00:58:18.350 --> 00:58:20.130
if we take a look at this,

00:58:20.130 --> 00:58:22.500
we've got a bunch of different
columns here, DBH, height,

00:58:22.500 --> 00:58:26.983
volume, light, tree ID, and
number of leaves in the tree.

00:58:28.220 --> 00:58:30.170
So we can use select to select

00:58:30.170 --> 00:58:31.280
which columns we want to keep.

00:58:31.280 --> 00:58:35.490
Let's say we're only
interested in DBH and height

00:58:35.490 --> 00:58:37.560
and tree ID.

00:58:37.560 --> 00:58:42.393
So we can just say DBH, height,

00:58:44.560 --> 00:58:46.100
tree ID.

00:58:46.100 --> 00:58:47.030
It's that simple.

00:58:47.030 --> 00:58:48.330
When we run that function,

00:58:49.780 --> 00:58:51.640
now we get a new data frame table

00:58:51.640 --> 00:58:54.723
that just consists of
DBH, height and tree ID.

00:58:55.600 --> 00:58:56.680
Now we could have also,

00:58:56.680 --> 00:58:58.410
let's say we wanted to keep everything,

00:58:58.410 --> 00:59:02.900
but we wanted to remove tree volume.

00:59:02.900 --> 00:59:06.340
So all we would have to
do is put in a minus sign

00:59:06.340 --> 00:59:07.353
and then volume,

00:59:08.520 --> 00:59:10.080
just like that.

00:59:10.080 --> 00:59:13.350
And now we remove tree
volume from this data frame.

00:59:13.350 --> 00:59:15.340
And let's say we wanted to
rename some of the columns

00:59:15.340 --> 00:59:16.330
in this data frame.

00:59:16.330 --> 00:59:18.040
Let's say tree leads is a little ambiguous

00:59:18.040 --> 00:59:21.023
and we wanna rename it to leaf number.

00:59:21.890 --> 00:59:26.117
We can just say leaf number equals.

00:59:28.360 --> 00:59:31.730
And then that column
was called tree leaves,

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:34.080
actually don't even
need those parentheses,

00:59:34.080 --> 00:59:35.123
tree leaves.

00:59:36.430 --> 00:59:40.273
So now we only want to remove volume,

00:59:41.390 --> 00:59:42.770
but we want to keep everything else

00:59:42.770 --> 00:59:46.730
and rename leaf number equals
tree leaves and run that.

00:59:46.730 --> 00:59:49.760
Now we have everything
except for volume and leaf

00:59:49.760 --> 00:59:52.640
and tree leaves is renamed to leaf number.

00:59:52.640 --> 00:59:55.980
Now, if we only included leaf
number equals tree leaves,

00:59:55.980 --> 00:59:58.740
that will only select
the column tree leaves

00:59:58.740 --> 01:00:00.233
and remove everything else.

01:00:02.930 --> 01:00:05.102
Oops, just like that.

01:00:05.102 --> 01:00:06.930
So make sure that you
include what you want to keep

01:00:06.930 --> 01:00:09.990
or what you want to remove and
then what you want to rename.

01:00:09.990 --> 01:00:11.800
So that's what the select function does.

01:00:11.800 --> 01:00:16.003
Then we have the filter function.

01:00:16.003 --> 01:00:16.836
And what this does

01:00:16.836 --> 01:00:20.130
is if select is to filter out
which columns you wanna keep,

01:00:20.130 --> 01:00:21.950
the filter function is to filter out

01:00:21.950 --> 01:00:24.070
which rows you want to keep.

01:00:24.070 --> 01:00:27.070
The first argument again is my data.

01:00:27.070 --> 01:00:29.140
And then it's simply a,

01:00:29.140 --> 01:00:32.970
any kind of a statement that
generates trues and falses

01:00:32.970 --> 01:00:36.600
based on the number of rows that you have.

01:00:36.600 --> 01:00:38.390
So we could say,

01:00:38.390 --> 01:00:40.090
let's take a look at all the data.

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:42.480
We could say,

01:00:42.480 --> 01:00:45.360
say we want to keep all the trees

01:00:45.360 --> 01:00:49.330
that had a DBH greater or less than 11.

01:00:49.330 --> 01:00:52.280
We want to keep only
these trees in our dataset

01:00:52.280 --> 01:00:53.400
that are less than 11.

01:00:53.400 --> 01:00:57.393
So we'll create this true
false statement and say,

01:00:58.290 --> 01:01:00.050
that's tree DBH.

01:01:00.050 --> 01:01:04.800
So we'll say we want to keep
everything where DBH equals

01:01:04.800 --> 01:01:07.660
actually is less than 11.

01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:09.290
So when we run that.

01:01:09.290 --> 01:01:12.470
We're left with just those rows

01:01:12.470 --> 01:01:15.490
and you can add multiple
statements on here.

01:01:15.490 --> 01:01:19.030
So we could say, we want everything where

01:01:20.439 --> 01:01:22.630
DBH is less than 11

01:01:22.630 --> 01:01:27.630
and height is greater than or equal to 70.

01:01:38.950 --> 01:01:40.570
So when we run that,

01:01:40.570 --> 01:01:43.710
now we have only those heights

01:01:43.710 --> 01:01:48.623
where the tree is greater than
70 and DBH is less than 11.

01:01:49.780 --> 01:01:53.090
And so note that every additional argument

01:01:53.090 --> 01:01:56.060
that you add on to the
filter function with a comma

01:01:56.060 --> 01:01:57.710
is the same as saying and.

01:01:57.710 --> 01:02:01.870
So I want to filter for
everything where this is true

01:02:01.870 --> 01:02:03.710
and this is true.

01:02:03.710 --> 01:02:06.720
If you want an either or,

01:02:06.720 --> 01:02:07.993
instead of a comma,

01:02:08.860 --> 01:02:11.860
you can just use this vertical line.

01:02:11.860 --> 01:02:13.290
I'm not sure what it's called.

01:02:13.290 --> 01:02:15.490
On my keyboard it's right
above the return key

01:02:15.490 --> 01:02:16.810
when I hold shift down

01:02:16.810 --> 01:02:19.660
and what this says is I
want to filter all my data

01:02:19.660 --> 01:02:23.140
where this is true, or this is true.

01:02:23.140 --> 01:02:26.010
So when we do that, we
get a whole lot more data.

01:02:26.010 --> 01:02:28.800
Next. We have the mutate function.

01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:31.900
And this is used to create
new columns in your data frame

01:02:31.900 --> 01:02:34.270
or to modify existing ones.

01:02:34.270 --> 01:02:36.140
Again, all of these packages,

01:02:36.140 --> 01:02:38.390
all of these functions
use the dplyr package.

01:02:39.560 --> 01:02:43.670
So once again, first
argument is our data set

01:02:43.670 --> 01:02:45.620
and let's say, we want to, right now,

01:02:45.620 --> 01:02:48.060
our height is measured in feet,

01:02:48.060 --> 01:02:51.097
but let's say we want to
convert our height to meters.

01:02:51.097 --> 01:02:55.960
And so to do that, we have to
multiply the height by 0.3048.

01:02:57.260 --> 01:03:00.380
That's just the conversion
to convert it to meters.

01:03:00.380 --> 01:03:03.550
And so to create a new
column in our data set,

01:03:03.550 --> 01:03:05.610
we can name it whatever we want.

01:03:05.610 --> 01:03:09.673
So in this case, height, meters equals

01:03:11.870 --> 01:03:13.440
and then we'll just say height

01:03:15.140 --> 01:03:17.793
times 0.3048.

01:03:21.470 --> 01:03:23.440
It's as simple as that.

01:03:23.440 --> 01:03:25.430
This new column that we want,

01:03:25.430 --> 01:03:26.630
we can name it whatever we want,

01:03:26.630 --> 01:03:28.703
equals height times whatever.

01:03:30.250 --> 01:03:33.480
So now if we take a look at
our data height in meters,

01:03:33.480 --> 01:03:35.063
we have it right here.

01:03:36.370 --> 01:03:39.160
And so if we want it to add
a few different new columns,

01:03:39.160 --> 01:03:41.113
we can just hit a comma.

01:03:42.000 --> 01:03:47.000
And let's say, we want to do height in,

01:03:48.920 --> 01:03:51.150
we'll just call it height modified.

01:03:51.150 --> 01:03:56.150
And it'll be a height times
0.05, whatever that means.

01:03:58.280 --> 01:04:01.810
We can also hit return
here because R is not line

01:04:01.810 --> 01:04:05.610
or space sensitive for
certain things like this,

01:04:05.610 --> 01:04:07.170
to keep it a little bit more neat.

01:04:07.170 --> 01:04:08.330
I can hit return there.

01:04:08.330 --> 01:04:09.930
So then here we can clearly see

01:04:09.930 --> 01:04:12.850
what all the different columns
that we're creating are.

01:04:12.850 --> 01:04:16.000
And if we run this, now we
have two additional columns,

01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:18.923
height meters and height
mod that have been added on.

01:04:19.861 --> 01:04:21.360
And you can also modify the existing ones.

01:04:21.360 --> 01:04:22.780
Like let's say,

01:04:22.780 --> 01:04:24.290
let's say we don't want to rename height,

01:04:24.290 --> 01:04:28.430
but we just want to modify it
so that its height in meters.

01:04:28.430 --> 01:04:30.500
I'm gonna remove this.

01:04:30.500 --> 01:04:34.000
For simplicity, I'm just
gonna call that height.

01:04:34.000 --> 01:04:36.440
So now what it's gonna
do is it's gonna say

01:04:36.440 --> 01:04:37.860
in this column height,

01:04:37.860 --> 01:04:40.070
we're going to take height
and multiply it by that.

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:42.860
But because height is the same
name as the existing column,

01:04:42.860 --> 01:04:45.120
it will just replace the original values

01:04:45.120 --> 01:04:46.323
with these new ones.

01:04:47.720 --> 01:04:50.180
So now if we take a look under height,

01:04:50.180 --> 01:04:53.263
height has already been
converted to meters in this case.

01:04:54.500 --> 01:04:56.870
Okay. Moving on.

01:04:56.870 --> 01:05:00.543
We're going to use the summarize function.

01:05:02.970 --> 01:05:05.870
So this summarizes all
the different columns

01:05:05.870 --> 01:05:07.730
in your data frame.

01:05:07.730 --> 01:05:12.310
If we put in the, my data all again here,

01:05:12.310 --> 01:05:17.310
we have to enter in what
summary values do we want.

01:05:17.450 --> 01:05:21.680
So for example, maybe we want
to find out the mean height

01:05:22.962 --> 01:05:23.962
and we'll call that,

01:05:25.080 --> 01:05:28.630
and mean height will be
based on the mean height.

01:05:28.630 --> 01:05:29.803
So applying the,

01:05:30.860 --> 01:05:32.250
applying the function mean,

01:05:32.250 --> 01:05:36.110
which I'll go over in a little
bit to the column height.

01:05:36.110 --> 01:05:41.110
And then let's say we also
want to know the maximum girth

01:05:43.930 --> 01:05:48.930
or maximum DBH, and we'll
use the function max,

01:05:49.170 --> 01:05:50.750
which we'll also go over in a bit later,

01:05:50.750 --> 01:05:52.793
but it's pretty self-explanatory.

01:05:55.360 --> 01:05:56.983
So now, if we run this,

01:05:58.120 --> 01:06:03.120
you simply get this table with
mean height and maximum DBH.

01:06:04.800 --> 01:06:05.633
Now you might wonder

01:06:05.633 --> 01:06:07.850
if you're just creating summary
values for all the columns

01:06:07.850 --> 01:06:09.780
why create a table

01:06:10.620 --> 01:06:13.430
if there's only going to be
one row of these summary values

01:06:13.430 --> 01:06:16.595
in this case, the mean
height and the maximum DBH.

01:06:16.595 --> 01:06:18.450
And that's because
summarize can be combined

01:06:18.450 --> 01:06:21.710
with the group by function,
which is really neat.

01:06:21.710 --> 01:06:25.670
And the group by function
allows us to split the data set

01:06:25.670 --> 01:06:28.030
into individual groups.

01:06:28.030 --> 01:06:33.003
For example, let's say group by,

01:06:36.000 --> 01:06:38.980
let's group the data into
whether the trees were in the sun

01:06:38.980 --> 01:06:40.600
or the shade.

01:06:40.600 --> 01:06:42.580
So we'll just use group by,

01:06:42.580 --> 01:06:44.440
first argument's the data again.

01:06:44.440 --> 01:06:47.830
And we'll just say, we
want to group by light.

01:06:47.830 --> 01:06:49.860
That's the name of that column?

01:06:49.860 --> 01:06:50.693
Oops!

01:06:53.210 --> 01:06:56.510
So if we run that you'll
notice nothing really changes.

01:06:56.510 --> 01:06:57.560
It's the same data set,

01:06:57.560 --> 01:07:01.570
but up here we have this
thing that says groups,

01:07:01.570 --> 01:07:02.403
light,

01:07:02.403 --> 01:07:05.120
and the two tells us that
there's two different groups.

01:07:05.120 --> 01:07:07.330
In this case, there is just shade or light

01:07:07.330 --> 01:07:09.363
or shade or sun or whatever it was.

01:07:10.590 --> 01:07:12.680
So on its own, it doesn't really do much.

01:07:12.680 --> 01:07:15.610
But then if we take this grouped data

01:07:15.610 --> 01:07:18.163
and put it inside of
this summarized function,

01:07:19.310 --> 01:07:22.563
so I'm just gonna copy this over again,

01:07:26.250 --> 01:07:27.083
copy that.

01:07:27.083 --> 01:07:28.760
And in place of my data all,

01:07:28.760 --> 01:07:31.260
I'm gonna put in this group by function.

01:07:31.260 --> 01:07:33.520
So now my data all has been grouped

01:07:33.520 --> 01:07:36.270
and then we're putting it
into this summarize function.

01:07:39.879 --> 01:07:40.990
And now take a look

01:07:40.990 --> 01:07:44.790
and now we have one row for
each of the different groups

01:07:44.790 --> 01:07:46.360
that we had grouped the data by.

01:07:46.360 --> 01:07:50.130
So now we have the mean
height for shade trees.

01:07:50.130 --> 01:07:52.520
We have the mean height for sun trees

01:07:52.520 --> 01:07:56.920
and the maximum DBH for
shade trees and sun trees.

01:07:56.920 --> 01:07:58.720
And when you use the summarize function

01:07:58.720 --> 01:07:59.860
with the group by function,

01:07:59.860 --> 01:08:03.393
it actually automatically
removes the grouping afterwards.

01:08:04.420 --> 01:08:05.680
So you'll notice here,

01:08:05.680 --> 01:08:08.690
there's no remaining grouping in the data,

01:08:08.690 --> 01:08:11.927
but sometimes it's useful to ungroup

01:08:15.130 --> 01:08:16.700
with the data to make sure that

01:08:16.700 --> 01:08:18.330
something doesn't go wrong down the line.

01:08:18.330 --> 01:08:19.630
So for example,

01:08:19.630 --> 01:08:24.630
we could have grouped by my data group.

01:08:26.600 --> 01:08:29.440
So I'm just saving this
data set, this data frame,

01:08:29.440 --> 01:08:32.440
as its own new data frame that's grouped.

01:08:32.440 --> 01:08:34.763
Oops, well that grouped.

01:08:35.760 --> 01:08:38.820
If we take a look at this same data frame,

01:08:38.820 --> 01:08:40.480
but up here, we've got this indication

01:08:40.480 --> 01:08:41.830
that it's grouped by light.

01:08:43.230 --> 01:08:48.230
And then we can put that
into summarize and run that.

01:08:48.800 --> 01:08:50.710
And so here is the data set

01:08:50.710 --> 01:08:53.900
or the summary of that
data set based on light,

01:08:53.900 --> 01:08:56.640
but then we might want to do
something else with these data

01:08:56.640 --> 01:09:00.480
and we want to ungroup them
'cause they're still grouped.

01:09:00.480 --> 01:09:05.480
So we'll put them in this
ungroup and when you run that,

01:09:05.830 --> 01:09:07.820
oops, it's the same data set again,

01:09:07.820 --> 01:09:10.720
but you'll notice the grouping
variables have disappeared.

01:09:12.400 --> 01:09:15.250
And you can also use the group by function

01:09:15.250 --> 01:09:16.990
with the filter and mutate.

01:09:16.990 --> 01:09:21.537
So for example, if you
want to filter for all,

01:09:23.110 --> 01:09:27.870
if you want to remove or
extract only the tallest trees

01:09:27.870 --> 01:09:31.310
from the sun group and the shade group,

01:09:31.310 --> 01:09:34.170
if you just used max height.

01:09:34.170 --> 01:09:36.880
So which height equals
max height in this filter,

01:09:36.880 --> 01:09:38.350
you'd only get one tree value

01:09:38.350 --> 01:09:39.250
because it's the maximum,

01:09:39.250 --> 01:09:41.770
the tree with the tallest height
out of the whole data set.

01:09:41.770 --> 01:09:44.400
But if you group the
data first based on light

01:09:44.400 --> 01:09:47.640
and applied this filter,
it would actually extract,

01:09:47.640 --> 01:09:50.100
it would extract the tallest
tree from each of the groups

01:09:50.100 --> 01:09:51.560
that you had grouped by.

01:09:51.560 --> 01:09:54.450
So the group by function can
be really, really useful.

01:09:54.450 --> 01:09:56.960
All right, we are almost done.

01:09:56.960 --> 01:09:58.680
And actually we've covered
most of the functions

01:09:58.680 --> 01:10:00.730
in this last section on basic statistics,

01:10:00.730 --> 01:10:03.110
but I'll quickly go
over them one more time.

01:10:03.110 --> 01:10:06.540
We've got the LM or linear model function.

01:10:06.540 --> 01:10:09.360
This is the most basic function

01:10:09.360 --> 01:10:13.060
for doing any kind of
statistical analysis in R.

01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:15.870
It allows you to do everything from T test

01:10:15.870 --> 01:10:18.480
to a nova regression, multiple
regression, et cetera.

01:10:18.480 --> 01:10:21.930
They all fall in that same
category of simple linear models.

01:10:21.930 --> 01:10:23.520
And so a simple way,

01:10:23.520 --> 01:10:28.520
simple example of this would
be let's do a linear model

01:10:28.830 --> 01:10:30.820
of DBH

01:10:33.190 --> 01:10:34.530
is a function of,

01:10:34.530 --> 01:10:38.769
so remember this tilde
indicates is a function of,

01:10:38.769 --> 01:10:41.320
what's on left of it is the Y variable,

01:10:41.320 --> 01:10:43.940
and on the right of it
are the X variables.

01:10:43.940 --> 01:10:48.643
So let's say we want to model
DBH as a function of light.

01:10:50.270 --> 01:10:51.473
So the sunlight,

01:10:52.850 --> 01:10:54.750
and what else do we have to work with?

01:10:58.320 --> 01:11:01.573
As function of sunlight
and the number of leaves.

01:11:02.690 --> 01:11:04.490
So remember leaves were just random.

01:11:04.490 --> 01:11:06.130
We just created this random data set.

01:11:06.130 --> 01:11:07.930
So tree leaves shouldn't
actually really be

01:11:07.930 --> 01:11:10.200
an important predictor of DBH,

01:11:10.200 --> 01:11:12.770
but let's just see what happens.

01:11:12.770 --> 01:11:13.603
So,

01:11:16.640 --> 01:11:20.370
a simple model would be just
how is DBH a function of light?

01:11:20.370 --> 01:11:23.470
And then we add the data in here,

01:11:23.470 --> 01:11:24.453
my data all.

01:11:25.300 --> 01:11:27.820
So this would be the
simplest way to do this.

01:11:27.820 --> 01:11:29.860
And since light is two
different categories,

01:11:29.860 --> 01:11:32.110
you can kind of think of this as a T test.

01:11:32.110 --> 01:11:33.560
Is there a difference in DBH

01:11:33.560 --> 01:11:35.793
between these two different variables?

01:11:37.040 --> 01:11:39.443
And we'll save this as mod one.

01:11:40.840 --> 01:11:42.803
And so we take a look at mod one.

01:11:44.460 --> 01:11:46.410
It doesn't really give
us much to work with.

01:11:46.410 --> 01:11:48.670
It just gives us the
estimate for the coefficients

01:11:48.670 --> 01:11:50.200
in this model,

01:11:50.200 --> 01:11:51.983
but the summary function,

01:11:53.950 --> 01:11:57.360
not to be confused with the
summarize function from before,

01:11:57.360 --> 01:12:00.880
just summary will give us
a more detailed summary

01:12:00.880 --> 01:12:02.483
of these model results.

01:12:05.270 --> 01:12:10.070
So here we have the model
formula, the residuals,

01:12:10.070 --> 01:12:13.449
the coefficients, and then the P value,

01:12:13.449 --> 01:12:16.500
whether the intercept and
the slope in this case,

01:12:16.500 --> 01:12:19.060
the effect of light is significant

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:22.420
and a few other test
statistics and information,.

01:12:22.420 --> 01:12:24.190
And you should check out my other video

01:12:24.190 --> 01:12:27.240
on doing basic linear regression in R

01:12:27.240 --> 01:12:29.490
if you're interested in learning
more about how this works.

01:12:29.490 --> 01:12:31.010
I'm not going to go over the details now,

01:12:31.010 --> 01:12:33.950
just want to show you
how to use this function.

01:12:33.950 --> 01:12:36.380
Now we could have also added
in some more variables.

01:12:36.380 --> 01:12:40.683
So for example, we'll add in,

01:12:43.660 --> 01:12:44.930
so let's say we think tree leaves

01:12:44.930 --> 01:12:46.603
are also a predictor of DBH.

01:12:48.120 --> 01:12:50.290
It might actually be the other way around,

01:12:50.290 --> 01:12:51.730
but either way, let's run that

01:12:51.730 --> 01:12:53.623
and now take a look at the summary.

01:12:54.920 --> 01:12:57.810
And we have the same results as before

01:12:57.810 --> 01:13:00.470
with sunlight appearing to be significant,

01:13:00.470 --> 01:13:04.320
but then tree leaves do not
have a significant effect

01:13:04.320 --> 01:13:07.493
or association with DBH in this case.

01:13:09.710 --> 01:13:11.210
Now there's a lot of assumptions to this

01:13:11.210 --> 01:13:12.770
because it's a linear model,

01:13:12.770 --> 01:13:14.800
but again, I'm not going
over any of the stats here

01:13:14.800 --> 01:13:16.873
just wanted to show you how this works.

01:13:19.080 --> 01:13:21.430
Then we have the mean function

01:13:21.430 --> 01:13:22.890
which we've gone over already.

01:13:22.890 --> 01:13:24.940
I think I used it a bunch of times.

01:13:24.940 --> 01:13:28.420
It just calculates the
mean value from a vector.

01:13:28.420 --> 01:13:31.320
So if we use num vec here,

01:13:31.320 --> 01:13:34.270
the mean value of num
vec happens to be five.

01:13:34.270 --> 01:13:37.100
And if we have an NA value,

01:13:37.100 --> 01:13:39.300
we can't actually calculate the mean,

01:13:39.300 --> 01:13:40.560
cause it will just give us an NA,

01:13:40.560 --> 01:13:45.020
so remember you can also use
NA dot RM or NA dot remove

01:13:45.020 --> 01:13:46.470
equals true

01:13:46.470 --> 01:13:50.040
and then it will calculate the
mean after removing the NA.

01:13:50.040 --> 01:13:53.140
Then we have max and min.

01:13:53.140 --> 01:13:55.460
Let's actually use our data here.

01:13:55.460 --> 01:13:57.310
So let's see what the maximum

01:13:59.210 --> 01:14:00.243
height is,

01:14:01.490 --> 01:14:02.793
87 feet.

01:14:03.980 --> 01:14:05.520
Let's see.

01:14:05.520 --> 01:14:07.183
And then we have the minimum,

01:14:10.280 --> 01:14:13.343
the minimum tree height is 63 feet.

01:14:14.560 --> 01:14:16.000
And so on.

01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:19.902
Then we also have median,
which is different from mean,

01:14:19.902 --> 01:14:22.810
'cause it gives us the value

01:14:22.810 --> 01:14:25.737
where 50% of the data are below that value

01:14:25.737 --> 01:14:30.080
and the other 50% are above
that value in the data set.

01:14:30.080 --> 01:14:32.880
So our median value in this case

01:14:34.150 --> 01:14:35.540
with tree heights

01:14:37.920 --> 01:14:39.920
is 76.

01:14:39.920 --> 01:14:42.280
If we did our mean value, it
might be close to the median,

01:14:42.280 --> 01:14:44.830
but there are cases where
the median is more important

01:14:44.830 --> 01:14:49.290
or more informative than
the mean and vice versa,

01:14:49.290 --> 01:14:50.870
but let's just, out of curiosity sake,

01:14:50.870 --> 01:14:52.380
let's see what the mean value

01:14:54.100 --> 01:14:56.910
of tree Heights is, is also 76.

01:14:56.910 --> 01:14:58.350
All right, well that makes sense.

01:14:58.350 --> 01:15:01.350
Given enough data that fall
under normal distribution,

01:15:01.350 --> 01:15:04.310
the mean and median
should be the same thing.

01:15:04.310 --> 01:15:07.050
And finally, last but not least,

01:15:07.050 --> 01:15:10.020
the function that I think
is quite underrated.

01:15:10.020 --> 01:15:11.660
Very few people ever mention it,

01:15:11.660 --> 01:15:13.260
but I just love this function

01:15:13.260 --> 01:15:16.770
'cause it's a really quick
way to get a nice summary

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:19.387
of any categorical variables
that you have in your data.

01:15:19.387 --> 01:15:22.053
And that is the table function.

01:15:23.060 --> 01:15:26.200
So in our dataset here that
we've been working with,

01:15:26.200 --> 01:15:28.230
let's say we want to get a quick summary

01:15:28.230 --> 01:15:30.530
of how many trees were in the shade

01:15:30.530 --> 01:15:32.940
and how many trees were in the sun.

01:15:32.940 --> 01:15:37.620
We'll just apply this table
function with my data all

01:15:39.730 --> 01:15:40.703
to light.

01:15:42.150 --> 01:15:44.530
And we have this quick
output that shows us

01:15:44.530 --> 01:15:47.823
15 trees were in the shade
and 16 trees were in the sun.

01:15:48.720 --> 01:15:51.620
And if you have a more
complex categorical variables

01:15:51.620 --> 01:15:55.290
such as maybe you have a
column for different species

01:15:55.290 --> 01:15:57.010
and you're dealing with
hundreds of different species,

01:15:57.010 --> 01:15:58.940
maybe even just tens of different species,

01:15:58.940 --> 01:16:00.565
but you have a huge data set

01:16:00.565 --> 01:16:02.510
and you just wanna see
how many data points

01:16:02.510 --> 01:16:05.090
do you have available for each species.

01:16:05.090 --> 01:16:06.070
Then you would run that

01:16:06.070 --> 01:16:07.660
and you'd get this nice little table

01:16:07.660 --> 01:16:09.490
of the different species names

01:16:09.490 --> 01:16:12.803
and how many individual
observations you have for each.

01:16:13.690 --> 01:16:17.800
So that is it, that
concludes the functions

01:16:17.800 --> 01:16:22.353
that I covered in my essential
functions of R cheat sheet.

